window.REVISION_SUBJECTS.push({"config":{"brandIcon":"🕉️","brandSubtitle":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062)","brandTitle":"Hinduism (AQA RS A)","breadcrumb":"Religious Studies / AQA A / Hinduism","examBoard":"AQA GCSE","footerNote":"Original practice questions written to the published AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062) specification (Hinduism, 3.1.4) and its five-part question structure (1, 1, 4, 6 and 12 marks, from Summer 2026). They are not AQA past-paper questions. Model answers are indicative exemplars, not official AQA mark schemes; other valid answers are credited in the exam.","heroEyebrow":"Component 1 · Hinduism · Beliefs \u0026 teachings · Practices","markingSchemaId":"aqa-rs-generic","pageTitle":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A – Hinduism Revision","papers":[{"id":"Beliefs","label":"Beliefs \u0026 teachings","subtitle":"modules 1.1–1.7 (spec 3.1.4.1)","title":"Hinduism: Beliefs and teachings"},{"id":"Practices","label":"Practices","subtitle":"modules 2.1–2.7 (spec 3.1.4.2)","title":"Hinduism: Practices"}],"storageId":"aqa_rs_a_hinduism","subjectArea":"Religious Studies","subjectName":"Hinduism"},"questions":{"paper1":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-1-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman and the three features of the divine","question":"Which one of the following is the Hindu term for ultimate reality without qualities or form?\nA) Saguna Brahman\nB) Nirguna Brahman\nC) Avatara\nD) Prakriti","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Nirguna Brahman.\nNirguna means 'without qualities': Brahman as pure divine consciousness beyond form and description. Saguna Brahman is God with qualities, an avatara is a descent of God in form, and prakriti is matter.","evidence":["Nirguna = without qualities","Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 2.3.6 'neti, neti'"],"examTip":"Learn the pair nirguna/saguna – they are easy marks and are needed in longer answers.","answerTopic":"nirguna Brahman"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-1-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman and the three features of the divine","question":"Which one of the following describes the Paramatma?\nA) The divine present within the heart of every being\nB) The goddess of wealth\nC) The material world\nD) The fourth age of the universe","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A) The divine present within the heart of every being.\nParamatma ('Supreme Self' or Supersoul) is the feature of the divine 'within the heart', the second of the three features of the divine (Brahman, Paramatma, Bhagavan).","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 18.61","Bhagavata Purana 1.2.11"],"examTip":"Connect each feature of the divine with its 'location': everywhere, within the heart, beyond.","answerTopic":"three features of the divine"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-1-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman and the three features of the divine","question":"Give one of the three features of the divine in Hindu belief.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: Brahman – the divine everywhere as non-personal energy; Paramatma – the divine within the heart; Bhagavan – the divine beyond as a personal loving God.","evidence":["Bhagavata Purana 1.2.11"],"examTip":"A single accurate term is enough; don't waste time explaining a 1-mark answer.","answerTopic":"three features of the divine"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-1-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman and the three features of the divine","question":"Give one name for an eternal spiritual world in Hindu belief.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: Vaikuntha (the realm of Vishnu); Goloka (the realm of Krishna); Kailasa (the abode of Shiva); Brahman / the spiritual sky.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 15.6"],"examTip":"Spelling of Sanskrit names does not need to be perfect, but it must be recognisable.","answerTopic":"spiritual worlds"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-1-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman and the three features of the divine","question":"Explain two contrasting Hindu understandings of Brahman.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First understanding: Some Hindus, especially followers of Advaita Vedanta, understand Brahman as nirguna – ultimate reality without qualities, pure consciousness beyond any form or description. Shankara taught that Brahman alone is real and that the true self (atman) is identical with Brahman, so the goal is to realise this oneness through knowledge rather than to worship a separate God.\n\nSecond understanding: Other Hindus, especially Vaishnavas such as followers of Ramanuja or ISKCON, understand Brahman as ultimately saguna – a personal God with real qualities such as love and mercy, such as Vishnu or Krishna. For them the impersonal aspect is only one feature of God, and the highest goal is a loving relationship of devotion (bhakti) in which the soul remains distinct from God.","evidence":["Nirguna/saguna","Shankara – Advaita","Ramanuja – Vishishtadvaita","Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7 'Tat tvam asi'"],"examTip":"With 'contrasting', make sure the two points really differ – naming two schools (Advaita and Vaishnava) makes the contrast obvious.","answerTopic":"understandings of Brahman"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-1-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman and the three features of the divine","question":"Explain two ways in which belief that the divine is present within the heart influences Hindus today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Because God as Paramatma is believed to live in every being's heart, Hindus are encouraged to treat all living beings with respect and non-violence (ahimsa). Many Hindus greet others with 'namaste' – bowing to the divine in the other person – and some are vegetarian because animals also have the divine within them.\n\nSecond way: Belief that God is within means a Hindu can worship anywhere, not only in a temple. Many practise meditation or japa each day, turning inward to focus on the divine in the 'space of the heart', and believe that the inner God guides their conscience when they make moral decisions.","evidence":["Paramatma","Bhagavad Gita 18.61","Namaste","Ahimsa"],"examTip":"'Influence' questions need practical effects on how Hindus live – actions, attitudes, worship.","answerTopic":"divine within the heart"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-1-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman and the three features of the divine","question":"Explain two Hindu teachings about the three features of the divine.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: The divine is everywhere as non-personal Brahman, the energy or consciousness that pervades the whole universe. The Isha Upanishad begins, 'All this is pervaded by the Lord', teaching that nothing exists apart from the divine. This encourages Hindus to see the sacred in nature, people and animals.\n\nSecond teaching: The divine is also within the heart as Paramatma and beyond as a personal loving God, Bhagavan. In the Bhagavad Gita 18.61 Krishna says, 'The Lord dwells in the hearts of all beings', and later asks Arjuna to 'always think of me, become my devotee' (18.65). The Bhagavata Purana (1.2.11) explains that one Absolute Truth 'is called Brahman, Paramatma and Bhagavan', so for many Vaishnavas these are three ways of realising the same God, with the personal form seen as the most complete.","evidence":["Isha Upanishad 1","Bhagavad Gita 18.61, 18.65","Bhagavata Purana 1.2.11","Brahman / Paramatma / Bhagavan"],"examTip":"Name the text AND show what it teaches about the point you are making – that secures both source marks.","answerTopic":"three features of the divine"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-1-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman and the three features of the divine","question":"Explain two Hindu teachings about the relationship between the one divine reality and the many gods.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: Many Hindus believe there is one ultimate reality that is described in many ways. The Rig Veda states, 'Truth is one; the wise call it by many names' (1.164.46). So when Hindus worship Ganesha, Lakshmi or Shiva, they may see them as different names or aspects of the one Brahman, rather than separate rival gods.\n\nSecond teaching: The Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 3.9.1 records the sage Yajnavalkya being asked 'How many gods are there?' He begins with '3,306' and reduces the number step by step until he answers 'One', which is Brahman. This teaches that the many devas are powers or manifestations within one divine reality. Vaishnavas add that the devas are servants of the Supreme Lord, Vishnu or Krishna, who is their source.","evidence":["Rig Veda 1.164.46","Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 3.9.1–9","Devas as manifestations of Brahman"],"examTip":"Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 3.9.1 is named in the specification – learn the outline of the dialogue.","answerTopic":"one and many"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-1-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman and the three features of the divine","question":"'Brahman is best understood as a personal God.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nMany Hindus, especially Vaishnavas, would agree. In the Bhagavad Gita, the supreme reality speaks as Krishna, a person who befriends Arjuna, teaches him and asks for his love: \"Always think of me, become my devotee, worship me\" (18.65). A God who can love and be loved must be personal, so this suggests that the personal understanding is the most complete one.\n\nThe Bhagavata Purana teaches that the one Absolute Truth is known as Brahman, Paramatma and Bhagavan (1.2.11). Vaishnava teachers such as Ramanuja and, later, the Gaudiya tradition (including ISKCON) argue that these are stages of realisation, with Bhagavan – the personal God – as the fullest. Krishna even says, \"I am the basis of the impersonal Brahman\" (Gita 14.27). On this view, the impersonal Brahman is like the sunshine, while the personal God is the sun itself.\n\nA further argument is practical. Most Hindus in Britain worship through puja, arati and bhajans directed at a deity such as Krishna, Rama, Shiva or Durga. Devotion (bhakti) is the most widely practised path, and it needs a personal object of love, so the personal understanding is the one that actually shapes Hindu religious life.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nFollowers of Advaita Vedanta, following Shankara, argue that Brahman is ultimately nirguna – beyond all qualities, including personality. The Upanishads describe Brahman only as \"neti, neti\" – \"not this, not this\" (Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 2.3.6), because any description limits it. A \"person\" has a particular form and character, so calling Brahman personal makes the infinite finite.\n\nAdvaitins accept worship of a personal God (Ishvara) as a valuable stage that purifies the mind, but they teach that the final truth is \"Tat tvam asi\" – \"You are that\" (Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7): the self is identical with Brahman. If the self and Brahman are one, there is ultimately no separate person to worship.\n\nOthers might argue that no single understanding is \"best\". The three features of the divine (everywhere, within the heart and beyond) could all be equally valid, and the Rig Veda's \"Truth is one; the wise call it by many names\" suggests that different people approach the same reality in different ways according to their nature.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nBoth sides are rooted in Hindu scripture, so the answer depends on which tradition is followed. However, I think the statement is only partly true. The personal understanding is clearly the most influential in practice and is strongly supported by the Gita, but the existence of the nirguna tradition, and the teaching of the three features, shows that Hinduism itself does not insist on one \"best\" understanding. A justified conclusion is that a personal understanding is best for a devotee on the path of bhakti, while the impersonal understanding is preferred by those on the path of knowledge.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 14.27, 18.65","Bhagavata Purana 1.2.11","Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 2.3.6 'neti, neti'","Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7 'Tat tvam asi'","Rig Veda 1.164.46","Shankara / Ramanuja"],"examTip":"Use named schools (Advaita vs Vaishnava) to give your arguments a clear religious basis, and make the conclusion weigh the two sides rather than repeat them.","answerTopic":"personal or impersonal Brahman"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-1-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman and the three features of the divine","question":"'The many Hindu deities show that Hindus do not believe in one God.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nAt first sight Hindu worship involves many deities: Ganesha is honoured at the start of new ventures, Lakshmi at Diwali, Saraswati by students and Hanuman for strength and protection. Temples in Britain often have several shrines, each with its own murti and festivals, and the Vedas contain hymns to many devas such as Agni, Indra and Varuna. To an outsider this looks like belief in many gods.\n\nThe Brihadaranyaka Upanishad itself records the answer \"3,306 gods\" (3.9.1), and popular Hindu tradition sometimes speaks of \"33 crore\" (330 million) gods. Some villagers worship local goddesses who protect a particular place, with little thought of a single supreme being. So it could be argued that, in practice, many Hindus relate to many gods.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, the same Upanishadic passage continues: Yajnavalkya reduces the number of gods step by step until he answers \"One\" – Brahman (Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 3.9.9). The point of the dialogue is that the many gods are expressions of one divine reality. The Rig Veda says, \"Truth is one; the wise call it by many names\" (1.164.46).\n\nFor Vaishnavas, Vishnu or Krishna is the one Supreme Lord and the devas are his servants or powers; for Shaivas, Shiva is the supreme God; for Shaktas, the Goddess is the supreme reality. Each of these traditions is monotheistic in the sense that it worships one supreme God. Even the Tri-murti – Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva – is often explained as three functions (creating, preserving, destroying) of one divine reality.\n\nAdvaita Hindus go further, believing that all forms are manifestations of one nirguna Brahman. Many British Hindus, when asked, describe their faith as \"belief in one God who can be worshipped in many forms\", like one person who is a parent, child and friend at the same time.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe statement confuses the variety of Hindu worship with Hindu belief about ultimate reality. The scriptures most quoted by Hindus teach that there is one ultimate reality, and the main traditions each focus on one supreme God. The many deities are better understood as forms, aspects or servants of that one God. Therefore I disagree with the statement, while accepting that some popular practice can appear polytheistic and that Hinduism allows a wide range of interpretations.","evidence":["Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 3.9.1–9","Rig Veda 1.164.46","Tri-murti","Vaishnava, Shaiva, Shakta traditions"],"examTip":"Avoid calling Hinduism simply 'polytheistic' – show you understand the one/many relationship and the diversity of traditions.","answerTopic":"one God or many"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-2-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, male and female deities and avatara","question":"Which one of the following members of the Tri-murti is known as the preserver?\nA) Brahma\nB) Shiva\nC) Vishnu\nD) Ganesha","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C) Vishnu.\nVishnu preserves and sustains the universe and protects dharma; Brahma creates, Shiva destroys/transforms, and Ganesha is not part of the Tri-murti.","evidence":["Tri-murti: Brahma, Vishnu, Shiva"],"examTip":"Learn the three functions with their deities: create – Brahma, preserve – Vishnu, destroy – Shiva.","answerTopic":"Tri-murti"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-2-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, male and female deities and avatara","question":"Which one of the following deities is the goddess of knowledge and the arts?\nA) Lakshmi\nB) Saraswati\nC) Durga\nD) Parvati","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Saraswati.\nSaraswati is the goddess of learning, music and the arts, shown holding a veena; Lakshmi is goddess of wealth and fortune.","evidence":["Saraswati – knowledge, veena, swan"],"examTip":"Know one key role of each of the four deities named in the specification.","answerTopic":"deities"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-2-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, male and female deities and avatara","question":"Give one avatar of Vishnu.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: Rama; Krishna; Narasimha; Matsya; Kurma; Varaha; Vamana; Parashurama; Balarama (or Buddha in some lists); Kalki.","evidence":["Dashavatara"],"examTip":"Rama and Krishna are named in the specification – they are the safest answers.","answerTopic":"avatara"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-2-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, male and female deities and avatara","question":"Give one reason why Ganesha is worshipped.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: he is the remover of obstacles; he is worshipped at the start of new ventures, journeys or ceremonies; he is the god of wisdom and new beginnings; he is the son of Shiva and Parvati.","evidence":["Ganesha – Vighneshvara, remover of obstacles"],"examTip":"One brief, accurate reason is enough.","answerTopic":"Ganesha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-2-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, male and female deities and avatara","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in avatars influences Hindus today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Belief that God descends to the world gives Hindus hope and trust that God cares for them. Because Krishna promises in the Gita to appear 'whenever there is a decline of dharma', Hindus believe that good will triumph over evil, which encourages them to keep doing their duty even in difficult times.\n\nSecond way: Hindus try to follow the example of avatars in their own lives. Rama is seen as the ideal of dharma – a faithful son, loyal husband and just king – so many Hindus learn the Ramayana, celebrate his return at Diwali and try to show the same loyalty and righteousness in their families and work.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 4.7–8","Ramayana","Rama as maryada purushottama (ideal man)"],"examTip":"Each 'way' should say what Hindus actually do or feel because of the belief, then develop it with an example.","answerTopic":"avatara influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-2-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, male and female deities and avatara","question":"Explain two contrasting Hindu beliefs about the Tri-murti.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Many Hindus see the Tri-murti as three equal forms or functions of one divine reality: Brahma creates, Vishnu preserves and Shiva destroys so the universe can be renewed. On this view the three are like three roles of one God, showing that creation, life and destruction are all part of one divine cycle.\n\nSecond belief: Other Hindus do not see the three as equal. Vaishnavas believe Vishnu (or Krishna) is the Supreme Lord and that Brahma and Shiva act under him, while Shaivas believe Shiva is supreme and himself performs creation, preservation and destruction. So for these Hindus the Tri-murti describes functions within the universe rather than three equal Gods.","evidence":["Tri-murti","Vaishnavism","Shaivism"],"examTip":"Using Vaishnava and Shaiva views is a straightforward way to show contrast.","answerTopic":"Tri-murti views"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-2-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, male and female deities and avatara","question":"Explain two Hindu beliefs about avatara.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: An avatar is a 'descent' of God into the world to restore goodness. In the Bhagavad Gita 4.7–8, Krishna teaches, 'Whenever there is a decline of dharma… I manifest myself… to protect the good, to destroy the wicked and to re-establish dharma, I appear age after age.' So avatars such as Narasimha and Rama appear at times of crisis to defeat evil, showing God's care for the world.\n\nSecond belief: Avatars also reveal God's character so that humans can love and follow him. Rama, in the Ramayana, shows perfect dharma as a son and king, keeping his father's promise by accepting exile. Krishna reveals God's love and playfulness; many Gaudiya Vaishnavas follow the Bhagavata Purana (1.3.28), which teaches 'Krishna is the Lord himself', the source of all the avatars rather than just one of them.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 4.7–8","Ramayana","Bhagavata Purana 1.3.28","Dashavatara"],"examTip":"Bhagavad Gita 4.7–8 is the classic text for avatars – learn a short version of it.","answerTopic":"avatara"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-2-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, male and female deities and avatara","question":"Explain two ways in which Hindu deities show what the divine is like.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: The deities show that the divine has many qualities which people need in different areas of life. Lakshmi shows the divine as the giver of prosperity and good fortune; Saraswati as the source of knowledge and the arts; Ganesha as the one who removes obstacles. The Rig Veda (1.164.46) teaches 'Truth is one; the wise call it by many names', so each deity can be seen as revealing one aspect of the one reality.\n\nSecond way: Deities such as Hanuman show what a loving relationship with God is like. In the Ramayana, Hanuman serves Rama with total loyalty and courage, crossing the ocean to find Sita. This shows that the divine is approached through devotion (bhakti), and Hanuman is himself honoured as the perfect devotee – millions recite the Hanuman Chalisa by Tulsidas. Male and female deities together also show that the divine includes both masculine and feminine qualities, as in Shakti traditions.","evidence":["Rig Veda 1.164.46","Ramayana – Hanuman","Hanuman Chalisa (Tulsidas)","Shakti"],"examTip":"A Hindu text such as the Ramayana counts as a source of authority – name it and say what it shows.","answerTopic":"deities"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-2-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, male and female deities and avatara","question":"'Avatars are the most important way in which the divine presents itself.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nAvatars could be seen as the most important way the divine presents itself because they involve God actually entering the world to help it. In the Bhagavad Gita 4.7–8, Krishna promises that whenever dharma declines, \"I manifest myself… to protect the good, to destroy the wicked and to re-establish dharma\". This shows a God who acts in history, not a distant power.\n\nTwo of the most loved figures in Hinduism, Rama and Krishna, are avatars. Their stories, in the Ramayana and the Mahabharata/Bhagavata Purana, are the basis of major festivals such as Diwali, Holi, Rama Navami and Janmashtami. The Bhagavad Gita, perhaps the most widely read Hindu scripture, is the teaching of an avatar. For Gaudiya Vaishnavas (including ISKCON), Krishna is \"the Lord himself\" (Bhagavata Purana 1.3.28), so the avatar is the fullest revelation of God.\n\nAvatars also give clear moral examples. Rama shows how to follow dharma faithfully; Krishna teaches selfless action. A God in a human-like form is easy to understand, love and imitate.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, many Hindus worship deities who are not avatars. Shaivas worship Shiva as the supreme God, and Shiva is not normally thought of as descending in avatars in the same way. Goddess-worshippers (Shaktas) see Durga or Kali as supreme. Ganesha, Lakshmi, Saraswati and Hanuman are hugely popular, and Ganesha is worshipped first at almost every puja. For these Hindus the deities, not the avatars, are the main way the divine presents itself.\n\nOthers would argue that the most important presentation of the divine is within the heart. The Gita says that the Lord \"dwells in the hearts of all beings\" (18.61), so God is always present, not only at special times in history. Advaita Hindus would go further and say that all forms, including avatars, are ultimately ways of pointing to nirguna Brahman, which is beyond any form.\n\nFinally, the murti in temple and home could be seen as the most important presentation in everyday life, since that is where most Hindus actually meet God through darshan and puja.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nAvatars are clearly very important, especially for Vaishnavas, and they provide many of Hinduism's greatest stories and teachings. But Hinduism is diverse: Shaivas, Shaktas and Advaitins would all give a different answer. I therefore think the statement is too strong. It is more accurate to say that avatars are the most important way for Vaishnavas, while for Hinduism as a whole the divine is understood to present itself in many equally valid ways.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 4.7–8, 18.61","Bhagavata Purana 1.3.28","Ramayana","Shaivism / Shaktism / Advaita"],"examTip":"Show awareness that the answer differs between Vaishnava, Shaiva, Shakta and Advaita Hindus.","answerTopic":"avatara importance"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-2-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, male and female deities and avatara","question":"'The Tri-murti is the best way for Hindus to understand God.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nThe Tri-murti – Brahma the creator, Vishnu the preserver and Shiva the destroyer – helps Hindus understand that God is involved in every part of existence. Everything in the universe comes into being, lasts for a time and then ends, and the Tri-murti shows that each of these stages is in God's hands. This gives a complete picture of God's relationship with the world.\n\nIt also fits the Hindu understanding of time as cyclical. The universe goes through endless cycles of creation and dissolution, with the four ages (yugas) repeating. The Tri-murti explains this cycle in a simple, visual way, and is easy for children and new learners to grasp. It also teaches that destruction is not simply evil: Shiva's destruction clears the way for renewal and destroys ignorance, which is a positive message for people facing change or loss.\n\nFinally, the Tri-murti shows the unity behind the many: three forms but one divine reality, which reflects the Rig Veda's teaching that \"Truth is one; the wise call it by many names\" (1.164.46).\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, very few Hindus actually worship the Tri-murti as a group. Brahma has very few temples (Pushkar is the famous exception), while Vishnu and Shiva are worshipped in their own separate traditions. Vaishnavas believe Vishnu/Krishna is the Supreme Lord, and that Brahma is a created being who serves him; Shaivas believe Shiva is the supreme God who performs all three functions. For both groups, the Tri-murti is not the \"best\" way to understand God, because it seems to make their supreme God one of three equals.\n\nOther Hindus would say God is best understood through the three features of the divine – Brahman everywhere, Paramatma in the heart and Bhagavan beyond (Bhagavata Purana 1.2.11) – because this describes how a person can actually experience God, not just what God does to the universe. Advaita Hindus believe that God is ultimately nirguna, beyond all forms and functions, so the Tri-murti is at best a helpful picture.\n\nMany Hindus relate most deeply to God through a personal chosen deity (ishta-devata) such as Krishna, Durga or Ganesha, so the Tri-murti may have little effect on their devotion.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe Tri-murti is a helpful way to explain God's relationship with the cycle of the universe, especially to beginners. However, because it is rarely the focus of worship and because the main Hindu traditions each see one God as supreme, I do not think it is the best way for Hindus to understand God. For most Hindus, God is better understood through a personal relationship of devotion or through realising the divine within.","evidence":["Tri-murti","Rig Veda 1.164.46","Bhagavata Purana 1.2.11","Pushkar Brahma temple","Ishta-devata"],"examTip":"Developing a 'different view' with Vaishnava and Shaiva beliefs shows knowledge of diversity, which examiners credit.","answerTopic":"Tri-murti evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-3-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Matter (prakriti), the three qualities (tri-guna), illusion (maya) and cosmology","question":"Which one of the following is the guna of goodness and purity?\nA) Tamas\nB) Rajas\nC) Sattva\nD) Maya","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C) Sattva.\nSattva is goodness, light and purity; rajas is passion and activity; tamas is ignorance and inertia; maya is illusion, not a guna.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 14.5–9"],"examTip":"Learn the three gunas in order: sattva (goodness), rajas (passion), tamas (ignorance).","answerTopic":"tri-guna"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-3-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Matter (prakriti), the three qualities (tri-guna), illusion (maya) and cosmology","question":"Which one of the following is the present age in the Hindu cycle of four ages?\nA) Satya Yuga\nB) Treta Yuga\nC) Dvapara Yuga\nD) Kali Yuga","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"D) Kali Yuga.\nHindus traditionally believe we are living in Kali Yuga, the fourth and most degenerate age, which follows Satya, Treta and Dvapara.","evidence":["Four yugas: Satya, Treta, Dvapara, Kali"],"examTip":"The ages go from most to least virtuous.","answerTopic":"four ages"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-3-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Matter (prakriti), the three qualities (tri-guna), illusion (maya) and cosmology","question":"Give one meaning of the term prakriti.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: matter; material nature/energy; the material world from which bodies and minds are made; the 'lower' energy of God (Gita 7.4).","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 7.4"],"examTip":"'Matter' on its own is enough for one mark.","answerTopic":"prakriti"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-3-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Matter (prakriti), the three qualities (tri-guna), illusion (maya) and cosmology","question":"Give one type of being that Hindus believe lives in other worlds.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: devas (gods); asuras (demons); gandharvas (celestial musicians); nagas (serpent beings); apsaras; rakshasas; ancestors (pitris).","evidence":["Puranic cosmology – fourteen lokas"],"examTip":"Any recognisable inhabitant of the 'many worlds' gains the mark.","answerTopic":"many worlds"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-3-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Matter (prakriti), the three qualities (tri-guna), illusion (maya) and cosmology","question":"Explain two contrasting Hindu understandings of maya.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First understanding: In Advaita Vedanta, taught by Shankara, maya means that the world of separate, changing things is ultimately an illusion superimposed on Brahman, like a rope mistaken for a snake in poor light. Only Brahman is truly real, and liberation comes when knowledge removes this illusion and the self realises it is one with Brahman.\n\nSecond understanding: In Vaishnava traditions maya is the real energy of God which makes souls forget who they are. The world itself is real but temporary; the illusion is the false belief 'I am this body' and attachment to possessions. Krishna teaches that maya is hard to overcome, but those who surrender to him in devotion cross beyond it (Gita 7.14).","evidence":["Shankara – rope and snake","Bhagavad Gita 7.14","Advaita vs Vaishnava"],"examTip":"Use the rope-and-snake example to make the Advaita view clear and contrast it with 'real but temporary'.","answerTopic":"maya"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-3-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Matter (prakriti), the three qualities (tri-guna), illusion (maya) and cosmology","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in the three gunas influences Hindus today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Many Hindus choose their diet according to the gunas. Fresh vegetarian food such as fruit, grains, milk and vegetables is considered sattvic and helps a calm, pure mind, while meat, alcohol and stale food are seen as tamasic. So many Hindus are vegetarian and avoid intoxicants, especially before worship or on fast days.\n\nSecond way: The gunas influence how Hindus try to live and worship. A Hindu may get up early for meditation or puja (a sattvic time of day), keep good company and avoid anger and laziness, so that sattva grows in their character. The long-term aim is to rise beyond all three gunas, because the Gita says they bind the soul to the body (14.5).","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 14.5, 17.8–10","Sattvic food"],"examTip":"Practical examples – food, daily routine, company – make influence points detailed.","answerTopic":"gunas influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-3-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Matter (prakriti), the three qualities (tri-guna), illusion (maya) and cosmology","question":"Explain two Hindu teachings about the origins of the universe.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: The Nasadiya Sukta, the 'Hymn of Creation' in the Rig Veda (10.129), describes a time before creation when 'there was neither non-existence nor existence'. Verses 6–7 ask, 'Who really knows?… The gods came later than the creation of this world… He who oversees it in the highest heaven, he surely knows – or perhaps he does not know.' This teaches humility: the origin of the universe is a great mystery, and many Hindus feel this openness fits well with scientific theories such as the Big Bang.\n\nSecond teaching: The Puranas teach that creation comes from God and happens in endless cycles. Vishnu lies on the cosmic ocean, a lotus grows from his navel, and from it Brahma is born to create the worlds. At the end of each 'day of Brahma' the universe is dissolved and later created again, so time is cyclical rather than having a single beginning and end. The Bhagavad Gita (9.7–8) similarly says that at the end of a cycle all beings enter God's nature, and at the beginning he sends them forth again.","evidence":["Rig Veda 10.129.6–7 (Nasadiya Sukta)","Bhagavad Gita 9.7–8","Vishnu Purana / Puranas – cyclical creation"],"examTip":"Rig Veda 10.129.6–7 is named in the specification, so it is the best source to use for cosmology.","answerTopic":"cosmology"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-3-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Matter (prakriti), the three qualities (tri-guna), illusion (maya) and cosmology","question":"Explain two Hindu beliefs about the cycle of four ages.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Hindus believe time moves in cycles of four ages – Satya, Treta, Dvapara and Kali Yuga – in which goodness gradually declines. The Puranas, such as the Vishnu Purana and Bhagavata Purana, picture dharma as a bull that stands on four legs in Satya Yuga but only on one leg in Kali Yuga. The lengths of the ages are in the ratio 4:3:2:1, and together they make one maha-yuga of about 4.32 million years.\n\nSecond belief: Hindus believe we now live in Kali Yuga, an age of conflict, greed and ignorance, which traditionally began in 3102 BCE. This explains why there is so much suffering and wrongdoing. However, many teachers say that in Kali Yuga liberation is easier through simple devotion; the Bhagavata Purana (12.3.51) teaches that by chanting God's names in this age a person can be freed. At the end of Kali Yuga, the avatar Kalki is expected to appear and restore Satya Yuga.","evidence":["Bhagavata Purana 12.3.51","Vishnu Purana – dharma as a bull","Kalki avatar","Satya, Treta, Dvapara, Kali"],"examTip":"Dates and ratios are not required, but one specific detail makes the explanation 'detailed'.","answerTopic":"four ages"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-3-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Matter (prakriti), the three qualities (tri-guna), illusion (maya) and cosmology","question":"'Hindu teachings about the universe cannot be reconciled with modern science.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nSome Hindu accounts of the universe are clearly different from science. The Puranas describe fourteen worlds inhabited by devas, asuras, gandharvas and nagas, and picture Brahma being born from a lotus that grows from Vishnu's navel. Science has found no evidence of such worlds or beings, and it explains the universe through natural laws rather than gods.\n\nThe cycle of four ages teaches that human virtue has declined from a golden Satya Yuga to the present Kali Yuga. Science, by contrast, suggests humans evolved gradually from earlier species, and archaeology shows no evidence of an earlier perfect age. A literal reading of these teachings would conflict with evolution and history.\n\nSome might also argue that science only explains material causes, whereas Hindu teaching claims that the universe exists for spiritual purposes, such as allowing souls to work out their karma. This kind of claim cannot be tested scientifically.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nMany Hindus argue that their teachings fit science better than those of many other religions. The Rig Veda's Hymn of Creation (10.129) describes a state when \"there was neither non-existence nor existence\" and openly asks \"Who really knows?\" (verses 6–7). This humble questioning is similar to scientific enquiry, and the idea of creation emerging from a single undifferentiated state is often compared with the Big Bang.\n\nHindu cosmology works on vast timescales – a day of Brahma is about 4.32 billion years – which is surprisingly close to the scientific age of the Earth (about 4.5 billion years). The idea of cycles of creation and dissolution resembles some scientific models of the universe, and the belief in countless universes is similar to the idea of a multiverse. The astronomer Carl Sagan remarked that Hinduism is the only great faith whose timescales roughly match modern cosmology.\n\nMany Hindus also read the Puranic stories symbolically: the lotus from Vishnu's navel shows that creation depends on God, not that a giant flower literally grew. Hinduism has no single creed requiring a literal reading, and teachers such as Swami Vivekananda welcomed science as compatible with Vedanta.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nSome Hindu teachings, if read literally, are hard to reconcile with science, especially the many worlds and the decline of the ages. However, the key texts, such as Rig Veda 10.129, are open, questioning and compatible with scientific ideas, and Hindus are free to read stories symbolically. I therefore disagree with the statement: Hindu teachings and modern science can be reconciled by most Hindus, because they answer different questions – science explains how, while Hindu teaching explores why.","evidence":["Rig Veda 10.129.6–7","Day of Brahma ≈ 4.32 billion years","Fourteen lokas","Four yugas","Swami Vivekananda","Carl Sagan, Cosmos (1980)"],"examTip":"Strong answers separate literal from symbolic readings of Hindu teachings.","answerTopic":"Hinduism and science"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-3-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Matter (prakriti), the three qualities (tri-guna), illusion (maya) and cosmology","question":"'Belief that the world is maya means that Hindus should not care about the world.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nIn Advaita Vedanta, Shankara taught that the world of separate things is ultimately an illusion superimposed on Brahman, like a rope mistaken for a snake. If the world is not ultimately real, it could be argued that it is pointless to become attached to possessions, careers or even social problems, because they belong to the realm of illusion.\n\nThe Bhagavad Gita teaches that the material world is temporary and that \"even the highest worlds are places of repeated birth and death\" (8.16). Attachment to the world, created by maya and the gunas, binds the soul to samsara. Sannyasis (renunciates) give up home, family and possessions to seek liberation, suggesting that the religious ideal is to turn away from worldly concerns.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, most Hindu traditions do not teach that the world is unreal. For Vaishnavas, the world is the real energy of God (Gita 7.4); maya is the illusion that makes souls forget God and think \"I am this body\". The world is temporary, but it belongs to God and should be cared for. The Isha Upanishad begins, \"All this is pervaded by the Lord… enjoy it by renouncing; do not covet anyone's wealth,\" teaching responsible use rather than neglect.\n\nThe Gita does not tell Arjuna to withdraw from the world; it tells him to act. Krishna teaches karma yoga: \"You have a right to your actions, but never to their fruits\" (2.47). The aim is to give up selfish attachment, not to stop caring. Indeed, those who see the divine in all beings are said to rejoice \"in the welfare of all beings\" (Gita 5.25).\n\nIn practice many Hindus are deeply involved in the world: charities such as Sewa International carry out disaster relief, and Hindu environmental projects such as the Bhumi Project and cow protection at Bhaktivedanta Manor show care for nature. Gandhi, who regarded the Gita as his guide, worked tirelessly for justice. Even Advaitins teach that, until liberation, we must follow dharma in this world, and many Advaita monks of the Ramakrishna Mission run hospitals and schools – Swami Vivekananda taught that serving people is serving God.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe statement misunderstands maya. Even in Advaita, maya is a reason not to be selfishly attached, rather than a reason not to care, and in the theistic traditions the world is real and sacred. The Gita's teaching of selfless action and the example of Hindu charities show that the dominant Hindu view is to care for the world while remaining unattached to it. I therefore disagree with the statement.","evidence":["Shankara – rope and snake","Bhagavad Gita 2.47, 5.25, 7.4, 8.16","Isha Upanishad 1","Ramakrishna Mission / Vivekananda","Sewa International","Bhumi Project"],"examTip":"Distinguish 'unreal' from 'temporary' – this is the key to evaluating maya.","answerTopic":"maya and the world"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-4-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The atman; samsara, karma and moksha","question":"Which one of the following is the Hindu term for liberation from the cycle of rebirth?\nA) Samsara\nB) Moksha\nC) Karma\nD) Atman","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Moksha.\nMoksha is liberation from samsara; samsara is the cycle itself, karma is action and its consequences, and atman is the eternal self.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 8.15"],"examTip":"These four terms are the core vocabulary of the topic – learn precise one-line definitions.","answerTopic":"moksha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-4-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The atman; samsara, karma and moksha","question":"Which one of the following best describes the atman?\nA) The material body\nB) The eternal inner self\nC) The law of cause and effect\nD) The cycle of four ages","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) The eternal inner self.\nThe atman is the individual, eternal self, distinct from the material mind and body (Gita 2.20).","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 2.20"],"examTip":"Remember the specification wording: 'individual, eternal inner self, distinct from material mind and body'.","answerTopic":"atman"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-4-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The atman; samsara, karma and moksha","question":"Give one type of karma.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: sanchita (accumulated) karma; prarabdha (ripening, present-life) karma; kriyamana/agami (karma being created now or to come); good karma (punya); bad karma (papa).","evidence":["Sanchita, prarabdha, kriyamana"],"examTip":"'Good karma' or 'bad karma' is acceptable for one mark.","answerTopic":"karma"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-4-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The atman; samsara, karma and moksha","question":"Give one Hindu understanding of what happens at moksha.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: the atman merges with/realises its identity with Brahman; the soul goes to live eternally with God in the spiritual world; the soul is freed from rebirth; the soul enters loving service of God.","evidence":["Advaita and Vaishnava views of moksha"],"examTip":"Any accurate brief view of liberation gains the mark.","answerTopic":"moksha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-4-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The atman; samsara, karma and moksha","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in karma influences Hindus today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Because Hindus believe every action has consequences that return to them, they try to act morally and avoid harming others. For example, many Hindus are vegetarian because killing animals creates bad karma, and they try to be honest in business and kind to family members.\n\nSecond way: Belief in karma affects how Hindus respond to suffering. Many accept difficult circumstances calmly, seeing them as the result of past actions (prarabdha karma), rather than blaming God or others. However, they also believe they can improve their future by present good actions, so they are encouraged to give to charity, perform seva (selfless service) and worship regularly.","evidence":["Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 4.4.5","Prarabdha karma","Seva"],"examTip":"Develop each influence by giving a concrete action and linking it back to karma.","answerTopic":"karma influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-4-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The atman; samsara, karma and moksha","question":"Explain two contrasting Hindu beliefs about moksha.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Advaita Vedanta Hindus believe that moksha is the realisation that the atman is identical with Brahman. When ignorance is removed, the sense of being a separate individual disappears, like a drop of water merging with the ocean, and the self is one with ultimate reality.\n\nSecond belief: Vaishnava Hindus believe that in moksha the soul keeps its individual identity. It is freed from rebirth and lives eternally in the spiritual world, such as Vaikuntha or Goloka, in a loving relationship of service to God (Vishnu or Krishna). For them, liberation is reached mainly through devotion (bhakti) and God's grace rather than merging.","evidence":["Advaita – sayujya/merging","Vaishnava – Vaikuntha, Goloka","Drop and ocean analogy"],"examTip":"The 'merge vs. relationship' contrast is the clearest pair for this question.","answerTopic":"moksha contrast"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-4-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The atman; samsara, karma and moksha","question":"Explain two Hindu beliefs about the atman.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: The atman is eternal and cannot be destroyed. In the Bhagavad Gita 2.20 Krishna teaches, 'The self is never born, nor does it ever die… it is not killed when the body is killed.' This means that death is not the end of a person; only the body dies, which is why Krishna tells Arjuna not to grieve. It gives Hindus comfort when someone dies.\n\nSecond belief: The atman is distinct from the material body and mind, and it moves from body to body in samsara. The Gita (2.22) says, 'As a person puts on new garments, giving up old ones, the self accepts new bodies, giving up the old.' The Katha Upanishad (1.3.3–4) compares the self to the passenger in a chariot, while the body is the chariot and the mind the reins. This shows that the real person is the conscious self, not the body, so Hindus try to control the mind and senses rather than living only for physical pleasures.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 2.20, 2.22","Katha Upanishad 1.3.3–4 (chariot)","Atman distinct from mind and body"],"examTip":"Gita 2.20 and 2.22 are the two most useful quotations for atman and samsara – learn both.","answerTopic":"atman"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-4-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The atman; samsara, karma and moksha","question":"Explain two Hindu teachings about samsara and karma.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: Samsara is the cycle of birth, death and rebirth through which the atman passes. Bhagavad Gita 2.22 compares the soul to a person changing worn-out clothes for new ones. The soul may be reborn as a human, animal or in another world, and this cycle is seen as bondage because each life involves suffering, old age and death.\n\nSecond teaching: The kind of rebirth a soul receives is decided by the law of karma. The Brihadaranyaka Upanishad (4.4.5) teaches, 'As one acts, so one becomes: by good action one becomes good, by bad action bad.' Karma is not a punishment from God but a law of moral cause and effect, and present choices shape future lives. Because even good karma keeps the soul in samsara, the Gita (2.47) recommends acting without attachment to the results, so that no new binding karma is created and the soul can move towards moksha.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 2.22, 2.47","Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 4.4.5"],"examTip":"Show how the two ideas connect: karma decides the next birth in samsara.","answerTopic":"samsara and karma"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-4-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The atman; samsara, karma and moksha","question":"'The law of karma is a fair way of explaining why people suffer.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nMany Hindus would agree. The law of karma teaches that every intentional action has consequences which return to the person who performed it: \"As one acts, so one becomes\" (Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 4.4.5). This is fair because nobody suffers randomly or because of someone else's choices; each person receives the results of their own actions. It treats people as responsible moral agents.\n\nKarma also explains suffering that seems unfair within a single lifetime. A child born into poverty or with an illness has not done anything wrong in this life, but Hindus believe that suffering may come from prarabdha karma, the past karma ripening in the present. Across many lifetimes in samsara, everything balances out. This removes the problem of a loving God appearing to cause innocent suffering, because God is not punishing anyone.\n\nKarma also offers hope. Present choices (kriyamana karma) shape the future, so a person is never trapped; they can improve their future through good actions, devotion and seva. Many Hindus find this a fair and empowering way of dealing with hardship.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nCritics argue that it is not fair to suffer for actions that we cannot remember. If a person has no memory of a past life, they cannot learn from it, so the suffering seems pointless. It could also lead to blaming victims – for example, suggesting that disabled people or the poor \"deserve\" their situation. Historically, karma was sometimes used to justify the caste system and the treatment of Dalits, which most people today see as deeply unjust.\n\nSome suffering seems to come from the actions of others or from natural disasters affecting thousands at once, such as earthquakes or pandemics. It is hard to believe that every victim had exactly matching karma.\n\nMany Hindus themselves would say that karma is not the whole explanation. Suffering may also be a test, a way of developing compassion and detachment, or an opportunity to turn to God. In bhakti traditions, God's grace can remove karma: Krishna promises, \"I shall free you from all sinful reactions; do not fear\" (Gita 18.66). And Hindu ethics insist that, whatever the cause, others' suffering must be relieved – it is never an excuse to ignore it.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nKarma offers a consistent and, in principle, fair explanation of suffering, because it makes each person responsible for their own future. However, it becomes unfair if used to blame victims or to justify inequality, and many Hindus would accept that it is not the only explanation. I conclude that karma is a fair explanation when it is understood as a law of personal responsibility and combined with compassion, but not when it is used as a reason to accept injustice.","evidence":["Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 4.4.5","Prarabdha / kriyamana karma","Bhagavad Gita 18.66","Caste and Dalits"],"examTip":"Include a criticism from within Hinduism (e.g. against victim-blaming) as well as from outside.","answerTopic":"karma and suffering"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-4-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The atman; samsara, karma and moksha","question":"'The aim of moksha means that this life is not important.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nFor Hindus, the ultimate goal is moksha – liberation from samsara. Compared with eternal liberation, a single life is only one of countless lives, \"like changing worn-out clothes\" (Gita 2.22). If the atman is eternal and the body temporary, it could be argued that this particular life is not very important.\n\nThe Gita teaches that even the highest material worlds are places of repeated birth and death (8.16), so worldly success, wealth and pleasure cannot bring lasting happiness. Many sannyasis give up their families, homes and possessions to seek moksha, and Advaita teaches that the world of separate individuals is ultimately maya. These ideas suggest that this life matters only as a step on the way to something else.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, most Hindus believe this life is extremely important precisely because it is the means of attaining moksha. A human birth is seen as rare and precious because only humans can consciously pursue liberation through the paths of yoga. The choices a person makes now create karma that shapes their future, so wasting this life could mean many more births.\n\nThe four aims of life (purusharthas) show that Hinduism values this life: dharma (duty), artha (wealth) and kama (pleasure) are all legitimate goals alongside moksha. The ashrama system gives each stage of life – student, householder, retirement and renunciation – its own importance. The householder (grihastha) stage is often said to support society as a whole.\n\nThe Gita does not tell Arjuna to escape from life but to fight in the battle and fulfil his duty, acting without selfish attachment (2.47). Many Hindus also believe in jivanmukti – liberation while still living – which shows that moksha can be expressed in this life, through love and service. For devotees in the bhakti tradition, loving God now is itself the beginning of eternal life.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe goal of moksha does put this life into perspective: it is temporary, and material success is not the highest aim. But it does not make this life unimportant. Instead, Hindu teaching about the rare human birth, karma, the four aims and the Gita's call to action all show that this life is the essential opportunity to make spiritual progress. I therefore disagree with the statement.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 2.22, 2.47, 8.16","Four aims (purusharthas)","Ashramas","Jivanmukti"],"examTip":"Link the issue to other parts of the specification (four aims, paths of yoga) to build logical chains of reasoning.","answerTopic":"moksha and this life"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-5-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","question":"Which one of the following is the Hindu term for spiritual ignorance?\nA) Avidya\nB) Jnana\nC) Dharma\nD) Ahimsa","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A) Avidya.\nAvidya is spiritual ignorance – misunderstanding the true self. Jnana is knowledge, dharma is duty, ahimsa is non-violence.","evidence":["Avidya / vidya"],"examTip":"Avidya = a- (not) + vidya (knowledge).","answerTopic":"ignorance"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-5-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","question":"Which one of the following best describes Hindu belief about free will?\nA) Everything is fixed by fate\nB) People can choose their actions and create new karma\nC) Only priests have free will\nD) Free will ends at the age of sixteen","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) People can choose their actions and create new karma.\nPast karma shapes circumstances, but Hindus believe each person is free to choose how to act now (Gita 6.5).","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 6.5, 18.63"],"examTip":"Karma is not fate – this is a common misunderstanding.","answerTopic":"free will"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-5-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","question":"Give one Hindu explanation for why people suffer.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: past karma; ignorance (avidya) of the true self; attachment/desire for temporary things; suffering is a test or teacher; the effects of living in Kali Yuga; the actions of others using free will.","evidence":["Karma","Bhagavad Gita 2.62–63"],"examTip":"One reason only is needed.","answerTopic":"suffering"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-5-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","question":"Give one way in which Hindus believe spiritual knowledge can be gained.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: studying scripture such as the Upanishads or Bhagavad Gita; learning from a guru; meditation; jnana yoga; listening to discourses (satsang) and asking questions.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 4.34 (approach a teacher)"],"examTip":"Keep it brief.","answerTopic":"knowledge"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-5-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","question":"Explain two ways in which Hindu beliefs about suffering influence Hindus today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Many Hindus respond to their own suffering with acceptance and patience, believing it may result from past karma. Following the Gita's teaching to tolerate pleasure and pain like passing seasons (2.14), they try to stay calm and not blame God, often turning to prayer, puja or the recitation of scripture for strength.\n\nSecond way: Hindus believe they should help relieve the suffering of others. Because the divine is in all beings, serving people is serving God, so many British Hindus take part in seva – for example volunteering on Sewa Day, giving food at temples, or supporting charities such as Sewa International which provides disaster relief.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 2.14","Seva","Sewa Day / Sewa International"],"examTip":"Balance an 'inner' response (attitude) and an 'outer' response (action) to give two distinct ways.","answerTopic":"suffering influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-5-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","question":"Explain two contrasting Hindu views about the relationship between free will and God.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First view: Many Hindus emphasise that God does not interfere with free will. Each atman is responsible for its choices and creates its own karma; God, as Paramatma in the heart, only witnesses and advises. Krishna gives Arjuna his teaching and then says 'Do as you wish' (Gita 18.63), showing that people must choose for themselves.\n\nSecond view: Other Hindus, especially in some bhakti traditions, stress that everything ultimately depends on God, who directs all beings 'as if mounted on a machine' (Gita 18.61) and grants liberation by his grace. For them, human free will is real but limited; the most important free choice is to surrender to God, who then takes responsibility for the devotee (Gita 18.66).","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 18.61, 18.63, 18.66"],"examTip":"Two views from different Hindu emphases count as 'contrasting'.","answerTopic":"free will and God"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-5-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","question":"Explain two Hindu teachings about knowledge and ignorance.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: Ignorance (avidya) is the root cause of samsara and suffering. It means wrongly identifying with the body and mind instead of the eternal atman. The Gita (2.62–63) explains that dwelling on sense objects leads to attachment, desire, anger and delusion, and finally to ruin. Because people do not know who they really are, they chase temporary pleasures and create karma that keeps them in the cycle of rebirth.\n\nSecond teaching: Spiritual knowledge (jnana or vidya) removes ignorance and leads towards moksha. Krishna says, 'There is no purifier in this world like knowledge' (Gita 4.38), and that knowledge destroys ignorance as the sun lights up everything (5.16). The prayer of the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad (1.3.28) asks, 'Lead me from the unreal to the real, from darkness to light, from death to immortality.' Hindus gain such knowledge by studying scripture under a guru (Gita 4.34) and through meditation.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 2.62–63, 4.34, 4.38, 5.16","Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.3.28 'Asato ma sad gamaya'"],"examTip":"'Asato ma sad gamaya' is a short, memorable source – useful for several topics.","answerTopic":"knowledge and ignorance"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-5-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","question":"Explain two Hindu teachings about free will.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: Each person is responsible for their own spiritual progress. The Bhagavad Gita (6.5) teaches, 'Let a person lift themselves up by the self; let them not degrade themselves. The self alone is the friend of the self, and the self alone is its enemy.' This means humans cannot blame others or God for their choices; by controlling the mind and senses they can rise spiritually.\n\nSecond teaching: Free will works within the law of karma. Past karma (prarabdha) shapes the circumstances into which a person is born, such as their family and health, but they freely choose how to act now, creating new karma (kriyamana). The Brihadaranyaka Upanishad (4.4.5) says 'As one acts, so one becomes', showing that the future is shaped by present choices. This is why Hindus reject the idea that karma is simply fate.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 6.5","Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 4.4.5","Prarabdha vs kriyamana karma"],"examTip":"Explain the difference between past karma (circumstances) and present karma (choices) to show depth.","answerTopic":"free will"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-5-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","question":"'Suffering is always the result of a person's own karma.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nMany Hindus would argue that the law of karma applies to all experiences. The Brihadaranyaka Upanishad teaches, \"As one acts, so one becomes\" (4.4.5). If this law is universal, then every experience of pain or pleasure is ultimately a result of a person's own past actions, whether in this life or a previous one. Suffering that seems undeserved, such as a child born with an illness, can be explained by prarabdha karma – past karma ripening now.\n\nThis view also protects belief in a just universe and a good God. God does not cause suffering; each atman reaps what it has sown. Many Hindus find this a helpful response because it encourages them to accept hardships patiently and to focus on doing good so that their future is better.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, other Hindu teachings suggest that suffering also has other causes. Ignorance (avidya) and attachment are themselves said to be the root of suffering: the Gita (2.62–63) describes how desire leads to anger and delusion. Suffering can come from our wrong attitude to events, not just the events themselves – the same loss may cause despair to one person and acceptance to another.\n\nPeople also suffer because of other people's free choices. If someone is harmed by a criminal or by injustice, it seems more accurate to say that the wrongdoer is responsible. Many Hindus argue that saying victims \"deserve\" it is a misuse of karma, and it has been criticised for justifying caste discrimination and poverty.\n\nSome suffering can be understood as a test or a spiritual opportunity. In the Bhagavata Purana, Queen Kunti even prays for difficulties because they make her remember Krishna (1.8.25). In this view, suffering is allowed by God for a person's growth, not simply as a reaction to past deeds. Living in Kali Yuga, an age of decline, is also said to bring suffering to everyone.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nKarma is a central Hindu explanation for suffering, and it is accepted by most Hindus as part of the picture. But the word \"always\" makes the statement too strong. Hindu teaching also points to ignorance, the free will of others, the conditions of Kali Yuga and suffering as a means of spiritual growth. I therefore conclude that suffering is often, but not always, best explained by a person's own karma, and that whatever its cause, Hindus have a duty to relieve it.","evidence":["Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 4.4.5","Bhagavad Gita 2.62–63","Bhagavata Purana 1.8.25 (Kunti's prayer)","Prarabdha karma","Kali Yuga"],"examTip":"Words like 'always' invite you to argue for 'sometimes' – use them in your conclusion.","answerTopic":"karma and suffering"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-5-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","question":"'Ignorance, not wrongdoing, is the main problem for human beings.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nMany Hindu teachings put ignorance (avidya) at the root of the human problem. Humans suffer and remain in samsara because they misunderstand who they are, identifying with the temporary body and mind rather than the eternal atman. Wrongdoing comes from this mistake: because we think we are the body, we desire possessions and pleasures, become angry when we do not get them, and harm others (Gita 2.62–63). Remove the ignorance and the wrongdoing disappears.\n\nThis is especially true in Advaita Vedanta, where liberation comes through knowledge alone: realising \"Tat tvam asi\" – \"You are that\" (Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7). Shankara taught that no amount of good action can bring moksha, because action belongs to the world of maya; only knowledge removes ignorance. The Gita agrees that \"there is no purifier like knowledge\" (4.38) and that the fire of knowledge burns all karma to ashes (4.37).\n\nThe great Upanishadic prayer \"Lead me from darkness to light\" (Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.3.28) also shows that the main spiritual task is to move from ignorance to understanding.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nOthers argue that wrongdoing (adharma) is just as serious. The law of karma means that harmful actions themselves bind the soul and cause suffering for others. Someone may know perfectly well that something is wrong and still choose to do it because of greed or anger. Arjuna himself asks, \"What makes a person do wrong, even against their will?\" and Krishna answers that it is desire and anger (Gita 3.36–37), which suggests that the problem is also moral weakness, not just lack of knowledge.\n\nMany Hindus focus on dharma – right action – as the foundation of life. The avatars descend \"when dharma declines\" (Gita 4.7), which suggests that wrongdoing in the world is a central problem God himself responds to. In bhakti traditions, the main problem is forgetting and turning away from God; the solution is love and surrender, not just intellectual knowledge.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nHindu teaching suggests that ignorance and wrongdoing are closely linked: ignorance of the true self leads to selfish desire, which leads to wrongdoing, which creates karma and further ignorance. I think the statement is largely correct, because most Hindu schools see avidya as the root cause; however, wrongdoing is not secondary, as it causes real harm to others and must be overcome through dharma and devotion as well as knowledge. The best answer is that ignorance is the root, but wrongdoing is the fruit, and both must be addressed.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 2.62–63, 3.36–37, 4.7, 4.37–38","Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7","Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.3.28","Shankara"],"examTip":"Use a 'chain' (ignorance → desire → wrongdoing → karma) – AQA rewards logical chains of reasoning.","answerTopic":"ignorance and wrongdoing"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-6-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"Personal virtues including ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love","question":"Which one of the following virtues means non-violence?\nA) Ahimsa\nB) Ahankara\nC) Artha\nD) Arati","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A) Ahimsa.\nAhimsa means not harming any living being. Ahankara is ego/pride, artha is wealth (an aim of life) and arati is a form of worship.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 16.2"],"examTip":"All four options start with 'A' – read Sanskrit terms carefully.","answerTopic":"ahimsa"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-6-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"Personal virtues including ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love","question":"Which one of the following is described in the Bhagavad Gita as withdrawing its limbs, as a picture of sense control?\nA) A cow\nB) A tortoise\nC) A swan\nD) An elephant","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) A tortoise.\nGita 2.58 compares a wise person who withdraws the senses from sense objects to a tortoise drawing in its limbs.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 2.58"],"examTip":"Memorable images from scripture help you score source marks too.","answerTopic":"sense control"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-6-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"Personal virtues including ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love","question":"Give one way in which Hindus show respect to elders.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: touching the feet of elders (pranam/charan sparsh); greeting them with namaste; caring for elderly parents at home; asking for their blessings at important times; obeying and listening to them.","evidence":["Taittiriya Upanishad 1.11.2"],"examTip":"A practical example is enough.","answerTopic":"respect"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-6-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"Personal virtues including ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love","question":"Give one personal virtue named in Hindu teaching, other than ahimsa.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: respect; empathy; mind/sense control; humility; love; truthfulness (satya); compassion (daya); self-discipline; tolerance.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 16.1–3"],"examTip":"Learn the six named in the specification: ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love.","answerTopic":"virtues"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-6-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"Personal virtues including ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in ahimsa influences Hindus today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Many Hindus are vegetarian because of ahimsa. They believe animals have an atman and that killing them causes suffering and bad karma, so they avoid meat, fish and often eggs. Temple kitchens in Britain, such as those at Bhaktivedanta Manor or Neasden Mandir, serve only vegetarian food.\n\nSecond way: Ahimsa encourages Hindus to resolve conflict peacefully and to avoid harm in words and thoughts as well as actions. Following Gandhi's example of non-violent protest, some Hindus campaign peacefully for justice, and many try to avoid anger, insults or bullying in daily life and work.","evidence":["Ahimsa","Gandhi – satyagraha","Vegetarianism"],"examTip":"Include ahimsa in thought and word, not just physical action, for a fuller development.","answerTopic":"ahimsa influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-6-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"Personal virtues including ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love","question":"Explain two ways in which the virtue of mind and sense control influences Hindus today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Many Hindus fast on certain days, such as Ekadashi (the eleventh day of the lunar fortnight) or during Navaratri, avoiding grains or eating only once. This trains the senses to accept discipline and is believed to purify the mind and help them focus on God.\n\nSecond way: Hindus practise meditation, japa or yoga to steady the mind. A Hindu might chant a mantra on a mala of 108 beads each morning. This helps them stay calm and control anger or desire in everyday life, following the Gita's teaching that a controlled mind is 'the best friend' (6.6).","evidence":["Ekadashi fasting","Japa","Bhagavad Gita 6.6"],"examTip":"Name a specific practice (fasting, japa, meditation) for each 'way'.","answerTopic":"sense control influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-6-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"Personal virtues including ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love","question":"Explain two Hindu teachings about the virtues of respect and empathy.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: Hindus are taught to respect parents, teachers and guests as if they were God. The Taittiriya Upanishad (1.11.2) instructs, 'Treat your mother as God, treat your father as God, treat your teacher as God, treat your guest as God.' This is why many Hindus touch the feet of their elders, care for grandparents at home and offer hospitality to visitors.\n\nSecond teaching: Hindus should feel the joys and sufferings of others as their own. In the Bhagavad Gita (6.32) Krishna says that the highest yogi is the one who 'sees the pleasure and pain of all beings as if they were their own'. Because the same atman and the same God are present in all beings, empathy is a natural result of spiritual understanding, and it motivates Hindus to help those in need through seva and charity.","evidence":["Taittiriya Upanishad 1.11.2","Bhagavad Gita 6.32"],"examTip":"Each teaching can have its own source – that makes the source mark secure.","answerTopic":"respect and empathy"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-6-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"Personal virtues including ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love","question":"Explain two Hindu teachings about humility and love.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: Humility – freedom from pride and ego (ahankara) – is essential for spiritual progress. The Gita lists 'humility, freedom from pride, non-violence, tolerance and simplicity' as qualities of true knowledge (13.8). Pride makes a person think they are the controller, which strengthens attachment to the body. The saint Chaitanya taught that devotees should be 'more humble than a blade of grass', which is why Hindus bow before the murti and serve others.\n\nSecond teaching: Love (prema/bhakti) is the highest response to God. Krishna says, 'Whoever offers me with love a leaf, a flower, a fruit or water, I accept that offering' (Gita 9.26). This shows that God values sincere love more than expensive offerings, so even the poorest person can worship. Love of God then extends to all beings – the devotee is 'friendly and compassionate to all' (Gita 12.13).","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 9.26, 12.13, 13.8","Chaitanya – Shikshashtakam verse 3"],"examTip":"Gita 9.26 is very useful for worship questions too.","answerTopic":"humility and love"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-6-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"Personal virtues including ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love","question":"'Ahimsa is the most important Hindu virtue.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nAhimsa is often described as the highest dharma (\"ahimsa paramo dharmah\", a phrase found in the Mahabharata). It follows directly from central Hindu beliefs: if the eternal atman is present in all living beings, and God dwells in every heart (Gita 18.61), then harming any being is harming the divine. The Gita says the wise see \"with equal vision\" a brahmin, a cow, an elephant, a dog and an outcaste (5.18).\n\nAhimsa also affects every area of life. It is the basis of Hindu vegetarianism, cow protection and care for the environment, and it shapes how Hindus speak and think, not only how they act. It also prevents the creation of bad karma, helping the soul towards moksha. Mahatma Gandhi showed its power by using non-violence to resist British rule, inspiring movements around the world, including Martin Luther King's civil rights campaign. For Gandhi, ahimsa and truth were inseparable.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nOthers argue that love is the most important virtue. In the bhakti traditions, loving devotion to God is the goal of life, and Krishna says that he accepts even a leaf offered \"with love\" (Gita 9.26). Love of God naturally produces ahimsa, empathy and humility, so love is the root from which the other virtues grow.\n\nSome would say humility or self-control is most important, because pride and uncontrolled senses lead to every other wrong. The Gita lists humility first among the qualities of knowledge (13.8), and teaches that controlling the mind makes it \"the best friend\" (6.6). Without self-control, a person cannot practise ahimsa consistently.\n\nThere are also limits to ahimsa. The Gita itself is set on a battlefield and Krishna urges Arjuna to fight as his duty (dharma) as a warrior. Many Hindus accept self-defence or protecting the innocent as sometimes necessary, which suggests that dharma – doing one's duty – may be more important than ahimsa in some situations.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nAhimsa has a strong claim to be the most important Hindu virtue because it is rooted in the belief in the atman in all beings and it shapes daily life so widely. However, Hindu teaching treats the virtues as interconnected, and in bhakti traditions love is seen as the source of all the others. I conclude that ahimsa is the most visible and widely emphasised virtue, but love of God and all beings is arguably more fundamental, since it is what makes true non-violence possible.","evidence":["'Ahimsa paramo dharmah' (Mahabharata)","Bhagavad Gita 5.18, 6.6, 9.26, 13.8, 18.61","Gandhi","Arjuna's duty to fight"],"examTip":"Compare ahimsa with at least two other named virtues and with dharma to show breadth.","answerTopic":"ahimsa importance"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-6-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"Personal virtues including ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love","question":"'It is impossible to practise ahimsa in the modern world.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nAhimsa means not harming any living being in thought, word or action. In the modern world it seems almost impossible to live without causing some harm. Farming kills insects and destroys habitats, cars and planes contribute to pollution and climate change, and many everyday products – clothes, phones, medicines – may involve exploitation of workers or testing on animals. Even a strict vegetarian diet may rely on dairy farming, in which male calves are often slaughtered.\n\nConflict also makes ahimsa difficult. Countries keep armies, and individuals may face violence. The Bhagavad Gita itself shows Krishna telling Arjuna to fight in a war, which suggests that complete non-violence is not always possible or right. In a competitive society, harsh words and anger online and at work are common.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, Hindu teaching does not demand perfection; it asks people to minimise harm. Ahimsa begins with intention: the Gita teaches that what matters is acting with a pure mind and without selfish desire. A person who tries to reduce harm, rather than causing it deliberately, is practising ahimsa.\n\nMany Hindus practise ahimsa successfully today. Millions are vegetarian; some British Hindus choose ethically produced milk, such as from the cow-protection farm at Bhaktivedanta Manor, where cows are never slaughtered. Others take part in environmental projects such as the Bhumi Project, or support animal welfare. Ahimsa in word and thought – avoiding gossip, bullying and hatred – is possible for anyone, whatever the world is like.\n\nGandhi showed that ahimsa can work even on a national scale: his non-violent campaigns helped bring independence to India in 1947, and inspired others such as Martin Luther King. If non-violence could challenge an empire, it can certainly guide modern life.\n\nFinally, modern life also makes ahimsa easier in some ways: plant-based foods, ethical labels and information about supply chains help people make less harmful choices than ever before.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nIt is true that complete, perfect ahimsa is probably impossible, because almost every action causes some harm. But Hindu teaching treats ahimsa as an ideal and a direction of life, focused on intention and minimising harm. Because millions of Hindus practise it meaningfully today, through diet, speech, environmental care and peaceful action, I disagree with the statement: ahimsa is difficult but not impossible.","evidence":["Ahimsa in thought, word and deed","Bhumi Project","Bhaktivedanta Manor cow protection","Gandhi","Bhagavad Gita – Arjuna's duty"],"examTip":"Distinguish 'perfect' practice from sincere practice – this helps you reach a nuanced conclusion.","answerTopic":"ahimsa today"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-7-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The four aims of human life; the meaning of dharma, sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma","question":"Which one of the following is NOT one of the four aims of human life?\nA) Dharma\nB) Artha\nC) Kama\nD) Samsara","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"D) Samsara.\nThe four aims (purusharthas) are dharma, artha, kama and moksha. Samsara is the cycle of rebirth, from which moksha brings release.","evidence":["Purusharthas"],"examTip":"'NOT' questions are easy to misread – underline the word.","answerTopic":"four aims"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-7-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The four aims of human life; the meaning of dharma, sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma","question":"Which one of the following best describes artha?\nA) Pleasure\nB) Wealth and prosperity\nC) Liberation\nD) Eternal duty","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Wealth and prosperity.\nArtha is the aim of earning wealth and success honestly; kama is pleasure, moksha liberation, sanatana dharma eternal duty.","evidence":["Artha"],"examTip":"Learn a one-word English gloss for each aim.","answerTopic":"artha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-7-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The four aims of human life; the meaning of dharma, sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma","question":"Give one of the four varnas.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: brahmin (priests/teachers); kshatriya (rulers/warriors); vaishya (merchants/farmers); shudra (workers/servants).","evidence":["Rig Veda 10.90.12 (Purusha Sukta)"],"examTip":"A single varna name earns the mark.","answerTopic":"varna"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-7-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The four aims of human life; the meaning of dharma, sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma","question":"Give one meaning of the term sanatana dharma.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: eternal dharma/duty; the eternal way/law; a name for Hinduism; universal duties that apply to everyone; the eternal nature/duty of the soul.","evidence":["Sanatana dharma"],"examTip":"'Eternal duty' or 'eternal way' is enough.","answerTopic":"sanatana dharma"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-7-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The four aims of human life; the meaning of dharma, sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma","question":"Explain two ways in which the four aims of life influence Hindus today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Because artha is a legitimate aim, many Hindus work hard to earn a good living, often running family businesses or studying for professional careers. However, because artha must be pursued within dharma, they try to earn money honestly and to use it to support their families, temples and charities rather than only for themselves.\n\nSecond way: Because moksha is the highest aim, Hindus try to include spiritual practice in everyday life even while pursuing work and family life. For example, a householder may perform puja at the home shrine every morning, fast on festival days and, in later life, spend more time on worship and pilgrimage, reflecting the vanaprastha stage of life.","evidence":["Purusharthas","Ashramas"],"examTip":"Show how dharma 'frames' artha or kama – it develops the point well.","answerTopic":"four aims influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-7-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The four aims of human life; the meaning of dharma, sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma","question":"Explain two contrasting Hindu views about varnashrama dharma.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First view: Some Hindus believe varnashrama dharma is a God-given order for society. They point to the Gita's teaching that God created the four varnas (4.13) and that it is better to do one's own duty than another's (3.35). On this view, each person contributes to society by fulfilling their role, like the different parts of one body in the Purusha Sukta.\n\nSecond view: Many other Hindus believe varna should depend on a person's qualities and actions, not their birth, and reject the hereditary caste system and untouchability as a corruption of Hindu teaching. Reformers such as Gandhi and Vivekananda campaigned against caste discrimination, and many British Hindus see caste as irrelevant to their faith today.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 3.35, 4.13","Rig Veda 10.90","Gandhi, Vivekananda","Untouchability abolished 1950"],"examTip":"Be accurate and respectful – distinguish varna (class by quality) from jati (caste by birth).","answerTopic":"varnashrama views"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-7-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The four aims of human life; the meaning of dharma, sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma","question":"Explain two Hindu teachings about the meaning of dharma.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: Dharma is the moral and cosmic order that upholds the universe, and when it declines God acts to restore it. The word comes from 'dhri', meaning 'to uphold'. In the Bhagavad Gita 4.7–8 Krishna says, 'Whenever there is a decline of dharma… I manifest myself… to re-establish dharma.' This shows dharma is not just human rules but part of how reality is meant to be.\n\nSecond teaching: Dharma is also each person's own duty, which depends on their nature and position in life. In the Gita (3.35) Krishna teaches, 'It is better to perform one's own dharma imperfectly than another's perfectly.' Arjuna, as a kshatriya, must fight to protect justice rather than run away. Hindus therefore believe that a student, a parent or a retired person each has duties suited to their stage of life (ashrama), and that fulfilling them creates good karma.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 3.35, 4.7–8","'dhri' – to uphold","Svadharma"],"examTip":"Show both meanings: dharma as cosmic order and as personal duty (svadharma).","answerTopic":"dharma"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-7-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The four aims of human life; the meaning of dharma, sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma","question":"Explain two Hindu teachings about the four aims of human life.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: Artha (wealth) and kama (pleasure) are good, but only when they are pursued within dharma. Krishna says, 'I am desire that is not contrary to dharma' (Gita 7.11). This means enjoying family life, food, art and success is not wrong; Hindus are encouraged to marry and raise families in the householder stage (grihastha), but they must earn money honestly and avoid pleasures that harm others or themselves.\n\nSecond teaching: Moksha is the highest of the four aims, because the others belong only to this temporary life. The Katha Upanishad (1.2.1–2) contrasts the 'good' (shreya) with the merely 'pleasant' (preya), and says the wise choose the good. So while Hindus value the first three aims, they believe that real fulfilment lies in liberation from samsara, which is why later stages of life (vanaprastha and sannyasa) focus increasingly on spiritual goals.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 7.11","Katha Upanishad 1.2.1–2 (shreya and preya)","Ashramas"],"examTip":"Linking the aims to the ashramas shows how the teaching works in practice.","answerTopic":"four aims"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-7-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The four aims of human life; the meaning of dharma, sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma","question":"'Moksha is the only aim of life that really matters.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nMoksha is described as the highest of the four aims (purusharthas) because it is the only one that is permanent. Wealth (artha) and pleasure (kama) belong to a body that will die, and even good karma only leads to another birth within samsara. The Katha Upanishad contrasts the \"good\" (shreya) with the merely \"pleasant\" (preya), and says the wise choose the good (1.2.2). If the purpose of life is to escape suffering permanently, moksha is the only aim that achieves it.\n\nThe Gita teaches that even the highest worlds are temporary (8.16), and Krishna says that those who reach him \"do not take birth again\" (8.15). Sannyasis give up the first three aims entirely to concentrate on liberation, and Advaita teachers such as Shankara saw the pursuit of knowledge for moksha as the true purpose of human birth.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, Hindu tradition deliberately gives four aims, not one. Dharma, artha and kama are seen as legitimate and necessary. Without artha, families could not be supported and temples and charities would not exist; without kama, there would be no marriage, children, art or joy. Krishna says, \"I am desire that is not contrary to dharma\" (Gita 7.11), suggesting that rightly ordered pleasure is itself sacred.\n\nDharma, in particular, could be seen as equally important, because it is the foundation of the other three and of moksha itself. A person cannot attain liberation without first living rightly. The Gita teaches Arjuna to perform his duty (3.35) rather than escape to seek liberation.\n\nThe ashrama system shows that different aims matter at different stages: a student focuses on learning dharma, a householder on artha and kama, and only later in life does moksha become the main goal. Most British Hindus are householders, for whom work, family and festivals really do matter, and they see them as part of a spiritual life, not distractions from it.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nMoksha is the ultimate aim, and in that sense it matters most. But \"the only aim that matters\" is too strong, because Hindu teaching insists that dharma, artha and kama are valuable and form the path towards moksha. I conclude that moksha gives meaning to the other aims, but they still matter – especially dharma, without which moksha is impossible.","evidence":["Katha Upanishad 1.2.2","Bhagavad Gita 3.35, 7.11, 8.15–16","Ashramas","Shankara"],"examTip":"Show how the aims are connected – the best answers argue that the other aims are a path towards moksha.","answerTopic":"moksha and the four aims"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-7-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The four aims of human life; the meaning of dharma, sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma","question":"'Varnashrama dharma has no place in Hinduism today.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nMany Hindus argue that the varna system has become linked with the hereditary caste system (jati), which has caused great injustice. For centuries, people born outside the four varnas (today called Dalits) were treated as \"untouchable\", denied entry to temples, education and wells. Reformers such as Gandhi, Swami Vivekananda and the Arya Samaj condemned this, and the Indian Constitution abolished untouchability in 1950. If varnashrama dharma leads to discrimination, it is against core Hindu values of ahimsa and seeing the divine in all beings (Gita 5.18).\n\nIn modern Britain, people choose careers according to their interests and abilities, not their birth. A Hindu may be a doctor, shopkeeper or soldier whatever their family background, and many young British Hindus regard caste as irrelevant. Many also see the ashrama stages – especially sannyasa – as impractical in a modern society where people work, retire and live with their families.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nOther Hindus argue that varnashrama dharma, properly understood, still has value. The Gita says the varnas are based on \"guna and karma\" – qualities and actions (4.13) – not birth. On this view, the idea that people have different natures and should contribute to society in different ways is sensible: some are naturally teachers, others leaders, businesspeople or skilled workers. Society, like the body in the Purusha Sukta (Rig Veda 10.90), needs all its parts.\n\nThe ashrama side of the teaching is also still practised. Hindus value the student stage of learning, the householder stage of supporting family and society, and many grandparents spend more time on worship, pilgrimage and service in later life (like vanaprastha). Some still become sannyasis, such as the monks of the Ramakrishna Mission or ISKCON.\n\nSvadharma – doing one's own duty well (Gita 3.35) – remains a central Hindu teaching, and it comes from varnashrama dharma. Parents, children and professionals each have responsibilities that suit them.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe hereditary caste system and discrimination have no rightful place in Hinduism today, and most Hindus would agree. However, the underlying principles of varnashrama dharma – doing duty according to one's nature and stage of life – are still widely valued. I therefore partly disagree with the statement: the abuse of varna as caste should be rejected, but the ideas of svadharma and the stages of life remain meaningful.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 3.35, 4.13, 5.18","Rig Veda 10.90 (Purusha Sukta)","Gandhi, Vivekananda, Arya Samaj","Indian Constitution Article 17 (1950)"],"examTip":"Handle caste sensitively and accurately: separate the ideal (varna by qualities) from historical practice (jati, untouchability).","answerTopic":"varnashrama today"}],"paper2":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-1-0","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Places of worship and their importance: home; temple; outdoors (such as shrines); the space of the heart","question":"Which one of the following is the inner sanctum of a Hindu temple where the main murti is kept?\nA) Garbhagriha\nB) Mandapa\nC) Havan kund\nD) Mala","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A) Garbhagriha.\nThe garbhagriha ('womb-house') is the shrine room holding the main murti. The mandapa is the hall for worshippers, a havan kund is a fire pit and a mala is a string of prayer beads.","evidence":["Garbhagriha"],"examTip":"Learn two or three parts of a temple – they are useful for 1-mark and 4-mark questions.","answerTopic":"temple"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-1-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Places of worship and their importance: home; temple; outdoors (such as shrines); the space of the heart","question":"Which one of the following best describes why the 'space of the heart' is a place of worship for Hindus?\nA) It is where the priest stands\nB) God is believed to dwell within the heart\nC) It is the name of a temple in Varanasi\nD) It is where prasad is kept","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) God is believed to dwell within the heart.\nHindus believe the divine lives within each being as Paramatma (Gita 18.61), so worship can be inward.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 18.61","Chandogya Upanishad 8.1.1"],"examTip":"This links worship to the belief in the three features of the divine.","answerTopic":"space of the heart"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-1-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Places of worship and their importance: home; temple; outdoors (such as shrines); the space of the heart","question":"Give one item found at a Hindu home shrine.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: murti or picture of a deity; lamp (diva); incense; bell; flowers; arati tray; water pot; kumkum/sandalwood paste; offerings of food; conch shell.","evidence":["Home shrine (mandir)"],"examTip":"Any single correct item gains the mark.","answerTopic":"home shrine"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-1-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Places of worship and their importance: home; temple; outdoors (such as shrines); the space of the heart","question":"Give one example of an outdoor place where Hindus worship.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: a shrine under a sacred tree (e.g. peepal); a roadside shrine; the banks of the River Ganges; a sacred hill such as Govardhan; the ghats at Varanasi; the sea or a river for murti immersion.","evidence":["Outdoor shrines"],"examTip":"Specific examples are better than vague ones.","answerTopic":"outdoor worship"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-1-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Places of worship and their importance: home; temple; outdoors (such as shrines); the space of the heart","question":"Explain two ways in which the home is important for Hindu worship.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: The home shrine is where most daily worship happens. Families perform puja and arati in the morning and evening, offering light, incense, flowers and food to the murtis before eating. This makes worship part of everyday life rather than something kept for special occasions.\n\nSecond way: The home is where Hindu faith is passed on to children. Children learn prayers, stories and how to perform puja from parents and grandparents, and important samskaras such as naming ceremonies, and festivals like Diwali, are celebrated at home. This keeps traditions alive, especially for British Hindu families living far from India.","evidence":["Home shrine","Samskaras","Daily puja and arati"],"examTip":"Develop each point with what actually happens at the home shrine.","answerTopic":"home worship"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-1-5","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Places of worship and their importance: home; temple; outdoors (such as shrines); the space of the heart","question":"Explain two ways in which Hindu temples in Britain are important for the Hindu community.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Temples are believed to be places where God is especially present, because the murtis have been installed with the prana pratishtha ceremony. Hindus visit to receive darshan, join arati led by priests and receive prasad, which they believe brings blessings; major festivals such as Diwali and Janmashtami attract thousands of worshippers, for example at Bhaktivedanta Manor.\n\nSecond way: Temples act as community centres for British Hindus. They provide classes in languages such as Gujarati or Hindi, dance and music lessons, youth groups and free vegetarian meals, and they host weddings and social events. This helps Hindus keep their identity and support each other, and temples such as Neasden Mandir also welcome school visits, improving understanding between communities.","evidence":["Prana pratishtha","Neasden Mandir","Bhaktivedanta Manor"],"examTip":"Use a named British temple to make your explanation detailed.","answerTopic":"temple importance"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-1-6","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Places of worship and their importance: home; temple; outdoors (such as shrines); the space of the heart","question":"Explain two reasons why the space of the heart is important for Hindu worship.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First reason: Hindus believe God lives in the heart of every being, so the heart is a true place of worship. In the Bhagavad Gita (18.61) Krishna says, 'The Lord dwells in the hearts of all beings.' This means that a Hindu does not need a temple or a priest to meet God; through meditation and remembering God they can worship anywhere, at any time.\n\nSecond reason: The space of the heart is where spiritual realisation takes place. The Chandogya Upanishad (8.1.1–3) describes a small lotus-shaped dwelling within the 'city of Brahman', the body, and within it a space as vast as the whole universe, containing heaven and earth. Yogis and Advaitins focus on this inner space in meditation to realise the atman and Brahman, and many Hindus see outer worship as a preparation for this inner worship.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 18.61","Chandogya Upanishad 8.1.1–3 (dahara akasha)"],"examTip":"Connect this practice back to the belief in Paramatma – that's a good way to develop it.","answerTopic":"space of the heart"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-1-7","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Places of worship and their importance: home; temple; outdoors (such as shrines); the space of the heart","question":"Explain two features of a Hindu temple and why they are important.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First feature: The garbhagriha (inner sanctum) contains the main murti, which has been installed by the prana pratishtha ceremony in which the deity is invited to be present. Above it rises the tower (shikhara), symbolising a sacred mountain such as Mount Meru or Kailash. This shows that the temple is understood as the home of God on earth, and worshippers come to receive darshan – to see and be seen by God.\n\nSecond feature: The temple has space for offerings and community worship, such as the mandapa (hall) where people gather for arati, bhajans and kirtan, and a kitchen where food is offered to God and given out as prasad. The Bhagavad Gita (9.26) teaches that God accepts 'a leaf, a flower, a fruit or water' offered with love, so offerings are central to temple worship. Sharing prasad also expresses the community's unity.","evidence":["Garbhagriha / shikhara","Prana pratishtha","Bhagavad Gita 9.26","Prasad"],"examTip":"A source of authority can explain why a feature matters – here Gita 9.26 explains offerings.","answerTopic":"temple features"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-1-8","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Places of worship and their importance: home; temple; outdoors (such as shrines); the space of the heart","question":"'Worship at home is more important for Hindus than worship in the temple.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nFor most Hindus, the home is where worship happens every day. Hinduism does not require weekly attendance at a temple, and most families have a home shrine where they perform puja and arati each morning and evening and offer food before meals. Because worship at home is daily, it arguably shapes a Hindu's life far more than occasional temple visits.\n\nThe home is also where faith is passed on. Children learn prayers, stories from the Ramayana and how to perform puja from parents and grandparents. Many samskaras and festivals, such as lighting divas at Diwali, take place in the home. In Britain, where some families live far from a temple, home worship is especially important for keeping Hindu identity.\n\nHindu teaching supports this. God is present everywhere and in the heart (Gita 18.61), so a temple is not essential, and Krishna accepts even \"a leaf, a flower, a fruit or water\" offered with love (Gita 9.26) – something anyone can do at home.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nOn the other hand, the temple is believed to be the home of God on earth in a special way. The murtis are installed by trained priests in the prana pratishtha ceremony, and elaborate rituals are performed several times a day. Receiving darshan of the deity in a temple, and taking prasad, are seen as powerful blessings that home worship cannot fully provide.\n\nThe temple also brings the community together. Large festivals such as Diwali, Navaratri and Janmashtami are celebrated with thousands of people at temples such as Bhaktivedanta Manor and Neasden. Temples hold weddings, classes in Hindu scripture and languages, and serve free food. For British Hindus, a minority in a mainly non-Hindu country, this shared worship strengthens identity and supports people.\n\nSome Hindus might say neither is most important, because the deepest worship is in the space of the heart through meditation and devotion.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nBoth places matter, but in different ways. The home is more important for daily, personal and family worship, while the temple is more important for community, festivals and priest-led rituals. Because Hinduism centres so much of religious life on the family and does not require temple attendance, I agree that home worship is more important for most Hindus – but the temple remains vital for the community as a whole.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 9.26, 18.61","Prana pratishtha","Bhaktivedanta Manor / Neasden Mandir","Home shrine"],"examTip":"Compare the two places using the same criteria (frequency, community, holiness) to build a balanced argument.","answerTopic":"home vs temple"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-1-9","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Places of worship and their importance: home; temple; outdoors (such as shrines); the space of the heart","question":"'Hindus do not need special places in order to worship.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nHindus believe that God is everywhere. The Isha Upanishad begins, \"All this is pervaded by the Lord\", and the Gita teaches that \"the Lord dwells in the hearts of all beings\" (18.61). If God is present everywhere and within every person, then any place can be a place of worship.\n\nThe specification itself includes the \"space of the heart\" as a place of worship. The Chandogya Upanishad (8.1.1–3) describes a space within the heart that contains the whole universe. Through meditation, japa and remembering God, a Hindu can worship on a bus, at work or in hospital. The great yogis and saints often worshipped in forests or caves without any temple.\n\nWorship also depends more on attitude than place. Krishna accepts a leaf, flower, fruit or water offered with love (Gita 9.26), showing that devotion, not location, is what matters. Some reform groups, such as the Arya Samaj, focus on simple fire worship (havan) rather than elaborate temples.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, special places help people worship. Most people find it hard to concentrate on God in ordinary surroundings, and a home shrine or temple creates a sacred atmosphere with murtis, lamps, incense and bells. The temple is believed to be a place where God is especially present, because the murti has been consecrated through prana pratishtha.\n\nSacred places such as Varanasi, the River Ganges or the site of the Kumbh Mela are believed to have special spiritual power. Millions of Hindus go on pilgrimage to them, believing that bathing in the Ganges purifies karma and that dying in Varanasi can bring moksha. This suggests that some places really are holier than others.\n\nSpecial places also bring the community together for festivals, weddings and teaching, which cannot happen in the \"space of the heart\".\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nHindu teaching clearly states that God can be worshipped anywhere, so in principle Hindus do not need special places. However, in practice special places – home shrines, temples and sacred sites – are extremely helpful and are believed to carry special blessings. I therefore agree that they are not strictly necessary, but they are very valuable, especially for beginners and for community life; the ideal is to combine outer worship with the inner worship of the heart.","evidence":["Isha Upanishad 1","Bhagavad Gita 9.26, 18.61","Chandogya Upanishad 8.1.1–3","Prana pratishtha","Varanasi / Ganges"],"examTip":"Use 'need' carefully – distinguish what is necessary from what is helpful.","answerTopic":"need for special places"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-2-0","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance; Shaivism and Vaishnavism","question":"Which one of the following is the Hindu fire ceremony?\nA) Arati\nB) Havan\nC) Darshan\nD) Japa","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Havan.\nHavan is the Vedic fire ceremony with offerings of ghee and grains into the sacred fire. Arati uses lamps waved before the murti, darshan is seeing the deity and japa is repeating a mantra.","evidence":["Havan – Agni"],"examTip":"Arati and havan both involve fire – remember havan is the fire ceremony with offerings into the fire.","answerTopic":"havan"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-2-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance; Shaivism and Vaishnavism","question":"Which one of the following is used by many Hindus when performing japa?\nA) A mala of 108 beads\nB) A conch shell\nC) A havan kund\nD) A rangoli pattern","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A) A mala of 108 beads.\nJapa is the repetition of a mantra, often counted on a mala of 108 beads.","evidence":["Japa mala"],"examTip":"Link each form of worship with an object used in it.","answerTopic":"japa"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-2-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance; Shaivism and Vaishnavism","question":"Give one way in which Shaiva worship differs from Vaishnava worship.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: Shaivas worship Shiva, Vaishnavas worship Vishnu/Krishna/Rama; Shaivas worship the lingam, Vaishnavas a human-like murti; Shaivas offer bilva leaves, Vaishnavas tulsi leaves; Shaivas wear horizontal ash lines, Vaishnavas a vertical tilak; different mantras ('Om Namah Shivaya' vs the Hare Krishna mantra).","evidence":["Shaivism / Vaishnavism"],"examTip":"Key differences in Shaiva and Vaishnava worship are named in the specification – revise at least three.","answerTopic":"Shaivism and Vaishnavism"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-2-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance; Shaivism and Vaishnavism","question":"Give one meaning of darshan.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: seeing the deity; seeing and being seen by God; viewing the murti in worship; receiving the blessing of seeing a holy person or place.","evidence":["Darshan"],"examTip":"'Seeing and being seen by God' is the most precise answer.","answerTopic":"darshan"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-2-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance; Shaivism and Vaishnavism","question":"Explain two contrasting ways in which Shaivas and Vaishnavas worship.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Shaivas usually worship Shiva in the form of the lingam, a rounded stone representing his formless and infinite power. They perform abhisheka by pouring water or milk over the lingam and offer bilva leaves, especially on Maha Shivaratri, when many fast and stay awake through the night chanting 'Om Namah Shivaya'.\n\nSecond way: Vaishnavas worship Vishnu or his avatars, especially Krishna and Rama, in human-like murtis that are bathed, dressed and decorated each day. They offer tulsi leaves and vegetarian food, which becomes prasad, and place great emphasis on bhakti through kirtan and chanting the Hare Krishna maha-mantra.","evidence":["Lingam / abhisheka / bilva","Murti / tulsi / prasad","Om Namah Shivaya / Hare Krishna maha-mantra"],"examTip":"Make sure each paragraph is clearly about one tradition and contrasts with the other.","answerTopic":"Shaiva and Vaishnava worship"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-2-5","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance; Shaivism and Vaishnavism","question":"Explain two ways in which arati is important for Hindus.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Arati expresses the belief that God is light, removing the darkness of ignorance. As the lamp is waved before the murti, worshippers sing praises such as 'Om Jai Jagdish Hare', showing love and honour to God as the most important guest.\n\nSecond way: Arati allows worshippers to receive God's blessing personally. After the lamp has been offered to the deity, it is brought to the worshippers, who pass their hands over the flame and then over their eyes and head, symbolically taking in the deity's light and blessing. It is usually performed in the morning and evening at home and in temples, uniting families and communities in worship.","evidence":["Arati","'Om Jai Jagdish Hare'"],"examTip":"Explain both meaning (symbolism) and practice for detailed points.","answerTopic":"arati"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-2-6","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance; Shaivism and Vaishnavism","question":"Explain two forms of Hindu worship and their significance.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First form: Puja is worship with offerings such as water, flowers, incense, light and food, made to a murti at home or in the temple. The deity is treated as an honoured guest. Its significance is shown in the Bhagavad Gita (9.26): 'Whoever offers me with love a leaf, a flower, a fruit or water, I accept that offering of love.' This teaches that God values the devotion behind the offering rather than its cost, so anyone can perform puja.\n\nSecond form: Havan is the Vedic fire ceremony, in which offerings of ghee and grains are placed into the sacred fire while mantras from the Vedas are chanted. The Rig Veda opens with a hymn to Agni (1.1.1), who is seen as the messenger carrying offerings to the divine. Havan is significant because it purifies the atmosphere and the participants, connects Hindus with the most ancient Vedic tradition, and makes God the witness at events such as weddings, where the couple walk around the sacred fire.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 9.26","Rig Veda 1.1.1 (hymn to Agni)","Puja / havan"],"examTip":"Choose two forms you can link to a text – puja (Gita 9.26) and havan (Rig Veda, Agni) work well.","answerTopic":"forms of worship"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-2-7","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance; Shaivism and Vaishnavism","question":"Explain two ways in which japa and kirtan help Hindus in their spiritual lives.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Japa, the repetition of a mantra on a mala of 108 beads, focuses and calms the mind. Many Hindus chant the Gayatri Mantra (Rig Veda 3.62.10), a prayer for the divine light to inspire the intellect, or 'Om Namah Shivaya'. Krishna says, 'Among sacrifices I am japa' (Gita 10.25), showing that this simple, quiet practice is a powerful form of worship that can be done anywhere.\n\nSecond way: Kirtan, the congregational chanting of God's names with music, increases love for God and builds community. Kirtan is usually call-and-response, with drums and cymbals, and is especially central in Vaishnava traditions such as ISKCON. The Bhagavata Purana (12.3.51) teaches that in Kali Yuga a person can be liberated simply by chanting the names of Krishna, so kirtan is seen as an easy and joyful path to moksha.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 10.25","Rig Veda 3.62.10 (Gayatri Mantra)","Bhagavata Purana 12.3.51"],"examTip":"Name a specific mantra – it shows detailed knowledge.","answerTopic":"japa and kirtan"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-2-8","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance; Shaivism and Vaishnavism","question":"'Worship is the most important part of being a Hindu.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nFor many Hindus, especially in the bhakti traditions, worship is the centre of religious life. Daily puja, arati, japa and kirtan express love for God, and the Bhagavad Gita teaches that God accepts even \"a leaf, a flower, a fruit or water\" offered with love (9.26). Krishna's final instruction is, \"Always think of me, become my devotee, worship me\" (18.65). If loving God is the goal of life, worship is the most important thing a Hindu can do.\n\nWorship also brings spiritual benefits. Darshan of the deity brings blessings, chanting God's names is said to purify the heart and bring liberation in Kali Yuga (Bhagavata Purana 12.3.51), and havan purifies people and places. For Vaishnavas, worship does not stop at moksha – liberated souls serve God eternally – so worship is not just a means but the goal itself.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nOther Hindus would argue that following dharma – living morally and fulfilling one's duties – is more important. The Gita teaches that Arjuna must perform his duty (3.35), and the law of karma means that actions towards others decide a person's future. A Hindu who performs elaborate puja but is dishonest or cruel would not be living according to Hinduism.\n\nAdvaita Hindus, following Shankara, see ritual worship as useful for purifying the mind but secondary to knowledge (jnana). Liberation comes from realising \"Tat tvam asi\" (Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7), not from rituals. Yogis may emphasise meditation in the space of the heart rather than outward worship.\n\nOthers stress seva (selfless service). Swami Vivekananda taught that serving humanity is serving God, and organisations such as the Ramakrishna Mission and Sewa International express Hinduism through practical help to others. Many British Hindus rarely visit a temple but see themselves as Hindu because of their values, family and festivals.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nWorship is clearly very important in Hinduism, and for bhakti Hindus it may be the most important part. However, Hinduism recognises several paths to God – action, knowledge, meditation and devotion – and dharma underpins them all. I therefore conclude that worship is one of the most important parts of being a Hindu, but not the only or necessarily the most important one; ideally, worship should be combined with moral living and service.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 3.35, 9.26, 18.65","Bhagavata Purana 12.3.51","Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7","Vivekananda – service to humanity","Different Hindu views about the importance of worship"],"examTip":"This question targets the specification's 'different Hindu views about the importance of worship' – use several distinct views.","answerTopic":"importance of worship"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-2-9","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance; Shaivism and Vaishnavism","question":"'Using a murti is more helpful in worship than meditation without images.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nMany Hindus believe a murti makes God present and accessible. After the prana pratishtha ceremony, the deity is believed to be present in the murti, so worshippers can see God (darshan), offer puja, wave the arati lamp and receive prasad. Vaishnavas call this the arca-avatara – God's \"descent\" into a form that can receive worship. This makes worship personal and loving, like serving an honoured guest.\n\nA murti also helps the mind concentrate. Most people find it very hard to focus on a formless God; seeing a beautiful image of Krishna, Shiva or Lakshmi, with its symbols, helps them remember God's qualities and stories. In the Gita, Krishna says that the path of the unmanifest is \"more difficult for embodied beings\" (12.5), suggesting that worship of God in form is easier and more helpful for most people.\n\nMurti worship is also active and involves the whole family and the senses – sight, sound, smell and touch – which makes it suitable for children and for community worship in temples.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nOther Hindus argue that meditation without images is more helpful. God is beyond any form; the Upanishads describe Brahman as \"neti, neti\" – \"not this, not this\" (Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 2.3.6). Advaitins and yogis focus on the divine within the space of the heart, believing that silent meditation leads more directly to realisation of the atman and Brahman. Patanjali's Yoga Sutras describe meditation (dhyana) leading to samadhi without needing an external image.\n\nSome Hindus reject murti worship altogether. The Arya Samaj, founded by Swami Dayananda Saraswati in 1875, taught that the Vedas do not support image worship and that God should be worshipped through havan and prayer. They worry that people may mistake the image for God, or focus on outward ritual instead of inner devotion and moral life.\n\nMeditation can also be practised anywhere, without the need for a shrine, priest or offerings.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nBoth forms of worship are accepted in Hinduism, and which is \"more helpful\" depends on the person. For most Hindus, especially those following bhakti, the murti is more helpful because it makes God personal and present. For those on the path of knowledge or meditation, worship without images may be better. I conclude that the murti is more helpful for most people, as the Gita itself suggests, but that meditation without images can lead further for those who are ready for it.","evidence":["Prana pratishtha / arca-avatara","Bhagavad Gita 12.5","Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 2.3.6","Yoga Sutras – dhyana, samadhi","Arya Samaj (Dayananda Saraswati, 1875)"],"examTip":"The Arya Samaj is a good example of diversity in Hindu views on worship.","answerTopic":"murti vs meditation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-3-0","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Focuses of worship and representations of the divine and their importance","question":"Which one of the following plants is especially sacred to Vishnu and Krishna?\nA) Bilva\nB) Tulsi\nC) Peepal\nD) Neem","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Tulsi.\nTulsi (holy basil) is worshipped as a goddess dear to Vishnu and Krishna; bilva leaves are offered to Shiva.","evidence":["Tulsi – Vrinda devi"],"examTip":"Pair plants with deities: tulsi – Vishnu/Krishna, bilva – Shiva.","answerTopic":"sacred plants"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-3-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Focuses of worship and representations of the divine and their importance","question":"Which one of the following is the ceremony that invites the deity's presence into a murti?\nA) Prana pratishtha\nB) Puja\nC) Parikrama\nD) Prasad","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A) Prana pratishtha.\nPrana pratishtha ('establishing the life-breath') is the consecration ceremony after which the deity is believed to be present in the murti.","evidence":["Prana pratishtha"],"examTip":"This term helps explain why the murti is treated as God, not just a statue.","answerTopic":"murti"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-3-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Focuses of worship and representations of the divine and their importance","question":"Give one sacred river in Hinduism.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: Ganga (Ganges); Yamuna; Saraswati; Godavari; Narmada; Kaveri; Sindhu.","evidence":["Sacred rivers"],"examTip":"Ganges is the safest answer.","answerTopic":"sacred rivers"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-3-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Focuses of worship and representations of the divine and their importance","question":"Give one reason why the cow is sacred to many Hindus.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: she gives milk and nourishment like a mother (gau mata); she represents non-violence and generosity; Krishna was a cowherd who loved cows; products of the cow (milk, ghee) are used in worship; the bull Nandi is Shiva's vehicle; ahimsa – cows are harmless creatures.","evidence":["Gau mata","Krishna as Govinda"],"examTip":"One reason only.","answerTopic":"sacred animals"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-3-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Focuses of worship and representations of the divine and their importance","question":"Explain two contrasting Hindu understandings of the murti.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First understanding: Many Hindus, especially Vaishnavas, believe God is truly present in the murti after the prana pratishtha ceremony. The murti is seen as a form in which God descends (arca-avatara) to accept worship, so it is treated like a living person – woken, bathed, dressed, offered food and put to rest each day.\n\nSecond understanding: Other Hindus see the murti as a symbol or aid to concentration rather than God himself. They believe God is beyond form, and the image helps the mind focus on God's qualities. Some, such as the Arya Samaj, reject murti worship altogether, believing it is not taught in the Vedas and that God should be worshipped through havan and prayer.","evidence":["Prana pratishtha","Arca-avatara","Arya Samaj"],"examTip":"Contrast 'God is present' with 'symbol only' (and 'rejected').","answerTopic":"murti views"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-3-5","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Focuses of worship and representations of the divine and their importance","question":"Explain two ways in which Hindus show reverence for sacred animals or plants.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Many Hindus grow a tulsi plant at home, often on a special stand in the garden or courtyard. Each morning or evening they water it, light a lamp before it and walk around it in prayer, because tulsi is honoured as a goddess dear to Vishnu and Krishna; tulsi leaves are offered in worship and tulsi wood is used for japa beads.\n\nSecond way: Hindus show reverence for the cow as gau mata (mother cow). Many do not eat beef, and some support cow-protection projects such as the herd at Bhaktivedanta Manor, where cows are never slaughtered and are cared for until they die naturally. At the festival of Gopashtami, cows are decorated and worshipped.","evidence":["Tulsi","Bhaktivedanta Manor cow protection","Gopashtami"],"examTip":"Concrete examples from British Hindu practice make points detailed.","answerTopic":"sacred plants and animals"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-3-6","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Focuses of worship and representations of the divine and their importance","question":"Explain two reasons why gurus and elders are important focuses of respect and worship for Hindus.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First reason: The guru is the spiritual teacher who removes ignorance and guides the disciple to God. The word is often explained as 'one who removes darkness'. Krishna teaches, 'Approach a spiritual master, enquire submissively and render service; the wise who have seen the truth will teach you' (Gita 4.34). This is why disciples honour their guru by touching their feet and celebrating Guru Purnima, and why many British Hindus belong to guru-led movements such as BAPS or ISKCON.\n\nSecond reason: Parents, grandparents and teachers are honoured because they give life, care and knowledge. The Taittiriya Upanishad (1.11.2) instructs, 'Treat your mother as God, treat your father as God, treat your teacher as God.' Hindus therefore touch the feet of elders to receive blessings, seek their advice on important decisions such as marriage, and care for them in old age.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 4.34","Taittiriya Upanishad 1.11.2","Guru Purnima"],"examTip":"Separate guru (spiritual teacher) and elders (family) into two clear reasons.","answerTopic":"guru and elders"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-3-7","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Focuses of worship and representations of the divine and their importance","question":"Explain two ways in which holy land, hills and rivers are focuses of worship for Hindus.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Rivers, especially the Ganga, are worshipped as goddesses. Hindus believe the Ganga descended from heaven, flowing from Vishnu's feet and through Shiva's hair, as told in the Ramayana and Puranas. Bathing in it is believed to wash away bad karma, and every evening a spectacular Ganga arati is offered on the ghats of Varanasi and Haridwar. Many Hindus scatter the ashes of the dead in the Ganges. In the Gita Krishna says, 'Among rivers I am the Ganga' (10.31).\n\nSecond way: Hills and sacred land are worshipped because of their links with God. In the Bhagavata Purana, Krishna lifts Govardhan Hill to protect the villagers of Vraj from Indra's storms; pilgrims now walk around the hill (parikrama) and, at the festival of Govardhan Puja (Annakut), a 'mountain' of food is offered in temples, including in Britain. Mount Kailash is honoured as the abode of Shiva, and India as a whole is seen by many as a sacred land.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 10.31","Bhagavata Purana – Govardhan lila","Ganga arati","Annakut"],"examTip":"Gita chapter 10 ('Among X, I am Y') is a rich source for sacred nature.","answerTopic":"holy land, hills and rivers"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-3-8","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Focuses of worship and representations of the divine and their importance","question":"'A murti is just a statue.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nFrom a non-religious point of view, a murti is made of stone, metal or wood by craftsmen and is physically no different from any other statue. It does not move, eat or speak, so to call it God seems mistaken.\n\nSome Hindus also hold this view, at least partly. The Arya Samaj, founded by Swami Dayananda Saraswati, taught that God is formless and that murti worship is not found in the Vedas; they worship through havan and prayer instead. Advaitins believe that ultimate reality is nirguna Brahman, beyond all form – \"neti, neti\" (Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 2.3.6) – so any image can only be a symbol. For them, the murti is at most a helpful aid to concentration.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, most Hindus would strongly disagree. In a temple, a murti is consecrated through the prana pratishtha ceremony, in which priests invite the life-breath and presence of the deity to dwell in it. From then on, it is no longer treated as an object but as God present in form. Vaishnavas call this the arca-avatara – God \"descending\" into a form that devotees can see and serve. The murti is woken, bathed, dressed, offered food and put to rest, just as one would serve a beloved guest.\n\nThis is based on the belief that God is everywhere and can make himself present wherever he wishes. If God is in the heart of every being (Gita 18.61), he can certainly be present in a murti. Krishna accepts offerings made with love (Gita 9.26), and darshan of the murti is a two-way meeting in which the devotee sees God and is seen by him.\n\nEven Hindus who see the murti as a symbol do not think it is \"just\" a statue. Like a national flag or a wedding ring, it carries deep meaning – Shiva's third eye, Ganesha's large ears and Lakshmi's lotus all teach about God's qualities and help worshippers focus.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nPhysically a murti is a crafted image, but for the great majority of Hindus it is far more than \"just a statue\": it is either God truly present in form or a sacred symbol that leads the mind to God. Only a minority, such as the Arya Samaj, reject its use. I therefore disagree with the statement, since within Hindu belief the murti's meaning and consecration make it holy.","evidence":["Prana pratishtha","Arca-avatara","Bhagavad Gita 9.26, 18.61","Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 2.3.6","Arya Samaj"],"examTip":"Avoid calling murti worship 'idol worship' – use the correct term and explain Hindu understanding.","answerTopic":"murti"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-3-9","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Focuses of worship and representations of the divine and their importance","question":"'Hindus should worship God alone, not deities, gurus or nature.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nMany Hindus believe there is one Supreme God, and some argue that worship should go to God alone. In the Bhagavad Gita, Krishna says that those who worship other gods \"are really worshipping me, but in the wrong way\" (9.23), and that worshippers of the devas go to the devas while his devotees come to him (9.25). This suggests that worshipping the Supreme God directly is better than worshipping lesser deities.\n\nThere are also risks in honouring gurus and nature. Some gurus have been exposed as dishonest or abusive, and treating any human being as divine can lead to blind obedience. Worshipping rivers or animals might seem to confuse what God has created with God himself. Reform movements such as the Arya Samaj encouraged Hindus to focus on the one formless God of the Vedas rather than on images and many deities.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, most Hindus see no conflict between worshipping God and honouring these other focuses, because they are understood as forms or reflections of God. The Rig Veda teaches that \"Truth is one; the wise call it by many names\" (1.164.46), so worshipping Ganesha or Lakshmi can be a way of worshipping the one reality. In chapter 10 of the Gita Krishna explains that he is present in the best of everything: \"Among rivers I am the Ganga… among trees I am the ashvattha\" (10.26, 10.31). Honouring a sacred river or tree is therefore honouring God's presence in it.\n\nThe guru is honoured because he or she leads the disciple to God. Krishna himself tells Arjuna to \"approach a spiritual master\" (Gita 4.34), and the Taittiriya Upanishad teaches respect for mother, father and teacher \"as God\" (1.11.2). This is not putting them in God's place; it is recognising God working through them.\n\nWorshipping nature also encourages care for the environment and ahimsa. Reverence for the Ganges has inspired clean-up campaigns, and reverence for cows supports cow-protection projects.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nIt is true that the Supreme God is the ultimate focus of Hindu worship, and that the honour given to gurus, deities and nature should not replace it. However, Hindu teaching sees God as present in all these focuses, so honouring them can be a way of worshipping God. I therefore disagree with the statement, while accepting that Hindus need wisdom in choosing gurus and should remember that all worship ultimately points to the one God.","evidence":["Bhagavad Gita 4.34, 9.23, 9.25, 10.26, 10.31","Rig Veda 1.164.46","Taittiriya Upanishad 1.11.2","Arya Samaj"],"examTip":"Bring in several focuses named in the specification (deities, guru, elders, rivers, plants, animals).","answerTopic":"focuses of worship"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-4-0","paper":"Practices","module":"2.4","moduleTitle":"Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today: Diwali and Holi","question":"Which one of the following stories is linked with the origins of Holi?\nA) Rama's return to Ayodhya\nB) Prahlad and Holika\nC) Ganesha's elephant head\nD) The churning of the ocean","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Prahlad and Holika.\nHoli remembers how Prahlad, devoted to Vishnu, was protected from the fire while Holika was burned. Rama's return to Ayodhya is linked to Diwali.","evidence":["Bhagavata Purana book 7 (Prahlad)","Holika Dahan"],"examTip":"Don't mix up the festival stories – Rama/Lakshmi for Diwali, Prahlad/Krishna and Radha for Holi.","answerTopic":"Holi origins"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-4-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.4","moduleTitle":"Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today: Diwali and Holi","question":"Which one of the following goddesses is especially worshipped at Diwali?\nA) Saraswati\nB) Parvati\nC) Lakshmi\nD) Ganga","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C) Lakshmi.\nLakshmi, goddess of wealth and good fortune, is welcomed into clean, lamp-lit homes during Diwali (Lakshmi puja).","evidence":["Lakshmi puja"],"examTip":"Remember that Lakshmi links Diwali to the topic of deities.","answerTopic":"Diwali"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-4-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.4","moduleTitle":"Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today: Diwali and Holi","question":"Give one way in which Hindus celebrate Diwali.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: lighting divas/lamps; drawing rangoli patterns; performing Lakshmi puja; cleaning the home; wearing new clothes; giving sweets and gifts; fireworks; visiting the temple; Annakut offerings; starting new account books.","evidence":["Diwali practices"],"examTip":"One practice is enough.","answerTopic":"Diwali practices"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-4-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.4","moduleTitle":"Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today: Diwali and Holi","question":"Give one meaning of the festival of Holi.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one of: victory of good over evil; God protects his devotees; celebrating Krishna's love for Radha; the arrival of spring/new life; equality and unity; forgiveness and renewing relationships.","evidence":["Holi meaning"],"examTip":"Meaning is not the same as practice – give a belief or message, not an activity.","answerTopic":"Holi meaning"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-4-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.4","moduleTitle":"Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today: Diwali and Holi","question":"Explain two ways in which Hindus in Great Britain celebrate Diwali.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Many British Hindu families celebrate Diwali at home by cleaning and decorating the house, drawing colourful rangoli patterns at the door and lighting divas in the windows. They perform Lakshmi puja to welcome the goddess of good fortune, believing she visits homes that are clean and bright, and exchange sweets and gifts with family and friends.\n\nSecond way: Hindus also celebrate as a community. In Leicester, tens of thousands of people gather on Belgrave Road's 'Golden Mile' for the Diwali lights switch-on and fireworks, one of the largest celebrations outside India. Temples such as Neasden Mandir hold Annakut on the Hindu New Year, offering hundreds of vegetarian dishes to the deities, which are then shared as prasad.","evidence":["Leicester Golden Mile","Neasden Annakut","Lakshmi puja","Rangoli and divas"],"examTip":"The question says 'in Great Britain' – include at least one British example.","answerTopic":"Diwali in Britain"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-4-5","paper":"Practices","module":"2.4","moduleTitle":"Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today: Diwali and Holi","question":"Explain two ways in which the festival of Holi is important for Hindus today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Holi reminds Hindus that good triumphs over evil and that God protects his devotees. On the night before Holi, bonfires (Holika Dahan) recall how Prahlad, who stayed devoted to Vishnu, was saved from the fire while the evil Holika was burned. This encourages Hindus to keep their faith in difficult times.\n\nSecond way: Holi brings people together and breaks down barriers. When people throw coloured powder at each other, everyone looks the same, so differences of age, caste, wealth and gender are forgotten for the day. This recalls the playful love of Krishna and Radha, and in Britain public Holi events often welcome people of all faiths, helping community cohesion.","evidence":["Holika Dahan","Krishna and Radha","Equality"],"examTip":"Link 'importance' to belief, community and identity.","answerTopic":"Holi importance"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-4-6","paper":"Practices","module":"2.4","moduleTitle":"Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today: Diwali and Holi","question":"Explain two origins of Diwali.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First origin: Diwali celebrates the return of Rama and Sita to Ayodhya after fourteen years of exile and Rama's victory over the demon king Ravana, as told in the Ramayana. The people of Ayodhya lit rows of lamps (deepavali) to welcome their rightful king home. Hindus remember this by lighting divas, which symbolise the victory of good over evil and dharma over adharma.\n\nSecond origin: Diwali is also linked with Lakshmi, goddess of wealth and good fortune, and in some regions with Krishna's defeat of the demon Narakasura. Hindus clean their homes and light lamps to welcome Lakshmi, and many businesses open new account books, praying for prosperity in the coming year. The lights also express the prayer of the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad (1.3.28), 'Lead me from darkness to light', showing that Diwali is about spiritual light – knowledge overcoming ignorance – as well as outward celebration.","evidence":["Ramayana – Rama's return to Ayodhya","Lakshmi","Narakasura","Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.3.28"],"examTip":"Questions on 'origins' need stories; questions on 'meaning' need beliefs – a strong answer links the two.","answerTopic":"Diwali origins"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-4-7","paper":"Practices","module":"2.4","moduleTitle":"Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today: Diwali and Holi","question":"Explain two origins of Holi.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First origin: Holi recalls the story of Prahlad, told in the Bhagavata Purana (book 7). His father, the demon king Hiranyakashipu, demanded to be worshipped as God, but Prahlad remained devoted to Vishnu. Holika, Prahlad's aunt, who was believed to be protected from fire, sat with him in a bonfire, yet she was burned and Prahlad survived; Vishnu then appeared as Narasimha to destroy the tyrant. Hindus light bonfires on the night before Holi (Holika Dahan) to celebrate the victory of devotion and goodness over pride and evil.\n\nSecond origin: Holi also remembers the playful love of Krishna for Radha and the gopis in Vrindavan, described in Vaishnava tradition and poetry. Krishna is said to have smeared colours on Radha and her friends. Throwing coloured powder at Holi recalls this divine love, and in Vrindavan and Mathura, and at ISKCON temples, Holi celebrations are full of kirtan and joy, expressing bhakti – loving devotion to God.","evidence":["Bhagavata Purana book 7 – Prahlad and Narasimha","Holika Dahan","Krishna and Radha in Vrindavan"],"examTip":"Two different stories make two different origins.","answerTopic":"Holi origins"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-4-8","paper":"Practices","module":"2.4","moduleTitle":"Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today: Diwali and Holi","question":"'Diwali and Holi are more about culture than religion for Hindus in Great Britain today.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nFor many British Hindus, especially young people, Diwali and Holi are mainly enjoyable cultural events. Diwali is associated with fireworks, new clothes, sweets, family meals and the Leicester lights switch-on, which attract many non-Hindus too. Holi has become popular as a \"festival of colours\", with public colour-throwing events at universities and parks where many participants know little about the religious stories behind it.\n\nFestivals also help British Hindus maintain their Indian, Gujarati, Punjabi or Tamil identity in a country where they are a minority. Food, music, dance and dress are central to the celebrations, and some Hindus who rarely worship at a temple still celebrate Diwali as a way of feeling connected to their heritage. Commercial interests – shops, events and advertisements – also emphasise the cultural side.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, both festivals are deeply rooted in Hindu scripture and belief. Diwali remembers Rama's return to Ayodhya and his victory over Ravana (Ramayana), and is a time of Lakshmi puja. The lamps express the prayer \"Lead me from darkness to light\" (Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.3.28), showing that the festival's meaning is spiritual: knowledge overcoming ignorance and dharma defeating adharma. Many families visit temples for darshan and arati, and Annakut at Neasden Mandir is an act of devotion, offering food to God.\n\nHoli recalls Prahlad's devotion to Vishnu and God's protection of the faithful (Bhagavata Purana, book 7), and Krishna's divine love for Radha. The Holika bonfire is a religious ritual, and Vaishnava temples celebrate with kirtan and worship. Even the colours and the breaking down of social barriers reflect the Hindu belief that the same atman is present in everyone.\n\nFinally, in Hinduism, religion and culture are hard to separate. Hinduism is often described as a \"way of life\" (sanatana dharma), so cooking for the family, giving sweets and visiting relatives can themselves be expressions of dharma, love and hospitality.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe cultural side of Diwali and Holi is clearly strong in Britain today, and some people celebrate them with little religious meaning. However, the festivals' origins, rituals and messages are thoroughly religious, and most Hindus see the cultural celebration as an expression of their faith rather than a replacement for it. I therefore disagree with the statement: for Hindus, the festivals are both cultural and religious, with the religious meaning at their heart.","evidence":["Ramayana","Bhagavata Purana book 7","Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.3.28","Leicester Diwali, Neasden Annakut","Sanatana dharma as a way of life"],"examTip":"Questions about 'Great Britain today' reward real British examples.","answerTopic":"festivals: culture or religion"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-4-9","paper":"Practices","module":"2.4","moduleTitle":"Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today: Diwali and Holi","question":"'Festivals are the best way for Hindus to pass on their faith to young people.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\n• refer to Hindu teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS SUPPORTING THE STATEMENT\n\nFestivals are exciting, colourful and fun, so they capture children's attention in a way that lessons often cannot. At Diwali, children help draw rangoli, light divas and hear the story of Rama and Sita from the Ramayana; at Holi they hear how Prahlad trusted Vishnu and was protected from the fire. These stories teach key Hindu values – courage, loyalty, devotion and the victory of good over evil – in a memorable way.\n\nFestivals also involve the whole family and community. Young people see grandparents, parents and temple communities worshipping together, take part in puja, arati and kirtan, and feel proud to be Hindu. In Britain, large public events such as the Leicester Diwali celebrations show young Hindus that their faith is valued by wider society, which can strengthen their identity.\n\nARGUMENTS SUPPORTING A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nHowever, festivals happen only a few times a year, whereas faith is passed on mainly through daily life. Hindu teaching places great importance on the home: children learn by joining daily puja at the home shrine, saying prayers before meals and watching how their parents treat others. The Taittiriya Upanishad tells children to honour \"mother as God, father as God, teacher as God\" (1.11.2), suggesting that family example and teaching are central.\n\nThere is also a risk that festivals focus on fun – fireworks, sweets and colours – so young people may enjoy them without learning their religious meaning. Deeper understanding requires study of scriptures such as the Bhagavad Gita, which teaches that knowledge comes from approaching a teacher with questions (4.34). Many British temples run weekly classes (e.g. Bal Vihar or Sunday schools), youth camps and Gita study groups, which may be more effective at building understanding.\n\nFinally, Hindus are taught that dharma is lived through actions. Seeing parents practise ahimsa, seva and honesty may teach young people more about being Hindu than any celebration.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nFestivals are a very powerful way of passing on Hindu faith because they make the stories and values memorable and create a sense of belonging. But they are most effective when combined with daily worship at home, the example of family members and proper teaching. I therefore conclude that festivals are an excellent way, but not the best on their own; the home is the most important place for passing on faith.","evidence":["Ramayana","Bhagavata Purana – Prahlad","Taittiriya Upanishad 1.11.2","Bhagavad Gita 4.34","Bal Vihar / temple classes"],"examTip":"Compare festivals with other ways of passing on faith (home, classes, example) rather than only describing festivals.","answerTopic":"festivals and young people"}]},"notes":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","title":"Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman and the three features of the divine","specFocus":"Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman; ultimate reality as divine consciousness (nirguna) and manifestation of God in form (saguna); spiritual worlds. Different understandings of the three features of the divine: everywhere, as non-personal (brahman); within the heart; beyond, as a personal loving God. Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 3.9.1.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"WHAT IS BRAHMAN?\nBrahman is the Sanskrit term for ultimate reality: the eternal, unchanging source and ground of everything that exists. The Upanishads describe it as sat-chit-ananda – being, consciousness and bliss. The Rig Veda states, \"Truth is one; the wise call it by many names\" (Rig Veda 1.164.46), which is why many Hindus see the many deities as ways of approaching one reality.\n\nNIRGUNA AND SAGUNA\n• Nirguna Brahman – Brahman \"without qualities\": pure divine consciousness, beyond form, gender, name or description. The Upanishads say it can only be described as \"neti, neti\" (\"not this, not this\", Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 2.3.6). This view is central to Advaita Vedanta, taught by Shankara (8th century CE), for whom Brahman alone is ultimately real and the true self (atman) is identical with it (\"Tat tvam asi\" – \"You are that\", Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7).\n• Saguna Brahman – Brahman \"with qualities\": God manifest in a form that can be known, loved and worshipped, e.g. Vishnu, Krishna, Shiva or the Goddess (Devi). Vaishnava teachers such as Ramanuja (Vishishtadvaita) and Madhva (Dvaita) taught that God is ultimately personal, with real qualities of love, mercy and beauty, and that souls remain distinct from God even in liberation.\n\nTHE THREE FEATURES OF THE DIVINE\nMany Hindus, especially Vaishnavas, describe one Absolute Truth understood in three ways (Bhagavata Purana 1.2.11: \"it is called Brahman, Paramatma and Bhagavan\"):\n1. Everywhere, as non-personal Brahman – the all-pervading divine energy (Isha Upanishad 1: \"All this is pervaded by the Lord\").\n2. Within the heart – Paramatma, the Supersoul who accompanies each soul as witness and guide (Bhagavad Gita 18.61: \"The Lord dwells in the hearts of all beings\").\n3. Beyond, as a personal loving God – Bhagavan, e.g. Krishna, who has a personality, form and relationships with devotees (Bhagavad Gita 9.29, 18.65).\nAdvaitins see the personal God as a lower, provisional understanding of nirguna Brahman; many Vaishnavas see impersonal Brahman as the \"light\" of the personal God (Bhagavad Gita 14.27: \"I am the basis of Brahman\").\n\nBRIHADARANYAKA UPANISHAD 3.9.1\nThe specification names this passage. The sage Vidagdha Shakalya asks Yajnavalkya, \"How many gods are there?\" He first answers \"3,306\", then steadily reduces the number – 33, 6, 3, 2, one and a half – until he answers \"One\" (3.9.9), identified with breath (prana) and Brahman. It shows the Hindu idea that the many gods are expressions of one divine reality.\n\nSPIRITUAL WORLDS\nBeyond the material universe many Hindus believe in eternal spiritual worlds where there is no birth, death or suffering: Vaikuntha (the realm of Vishnu), Goloka (the realm of Krishna) and Kailasa (Shiva's abode). Bhagavad Gita 8.21 and 15.6 describe a supreme abode \"from which, having attained, people do not return\".\n\nDIVERSITY AND TODAY\nHinduism has no single creed. A Smarta or Advaita Hindu may meditate on formless Brahman; an ISKCON devotee at Bhaktivedanta Manor (Watford) worships Krishna as the Supreme Person; a Shaiva sees Shiva as both formless (the lingam) and personal. Many British Hindus describe themselves as believing in \"one God with many forms\"."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","title":"How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, male and female deities and avatara","specFocus":"How the divine presents: the Tri-murti (Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva); male and female deities, including Ganesha, Lakshmi, Hanuman, Saraswati; the concept of avatara, including Krishna, Rama.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"HOW THE DIVINE PRESENTS\nHindus believe the one divine reality shows itself in many forms so that people can know, worship and relate to it. The specification focuses on the Tri-murti, male and female deities, and avatara.\n\nTHE TRI-MURTI (\"three forms\")\n• Brahma – the creator, who brings the universe into being at the start of each cycle. Shown with four heads (often linked to the four Vedas) and sitting on a lotus. He is rarely worshipped as a main deity; one famous temple is at Pushkar in Rajasthan. His consort is Saraswati.\n• Vishnu – the preserver or sustainer, who maintains the universe and protects dharma. Shown blue-skinned, holding a conch, discus (chakra), mace and lotus. His consort is Lakshmi. He descends as avatars.\n• Shiva – the destroyer or transformer, who ends the universe so it can be renewed, and destroys ignorance and ego. Shown as Nataraja (Lord of the Dance) within a circle of fire, or as a meditating yogi with a trident, third eye and the River Ganga in his hair. Often worshipped as the lingam. His consort is Parvati.\nThe Tri-murti expresses the cycle of creation, preservation and destruction. However, traditions differ: for Vaishnavas, Vishnu is the Supreme God and Brahma and Shiva are subordinate; for Shaivas, Shiva is supreme and performs all three functions.\n\nMALE AND FEMALE DEITIES\n• Ganesha – elephant-headed son of Shiva and Parvati; remover of obstacles; worshipped first at the start of any new venture, wedding or puja. His vehicle is a mouse.\n• Lakshmi – goddess of wealth, good fortune and prosperity; consort of Vishnu; worshipped especially at Diwali, when lamps are lit to welcome her into homes and businesses.\n• Hanuman – the monkey devotee of Rama in the Ramayana; symbol of loyal devotion, courage and strength. Many Hindus recite the Hanuman Chalisa for protection.\n• Saraswati – goddess of knowledge, learning, music and the arts; shown in white with a veena and a swan; honoured by students, especially at Vasant Panchami.\nGoddess-worship (Shaktism) sees the divine feminine (Shakti, Devi, e.g. Durga, Kali) as supreme power. Male and female deities together show that the divine includes both.\n\nAVATARA (\"one who descends\")\nAn avatar is a descent of God (usually Vishnu) into the world in a form. Krishna explains the purpose: \"Whenever there is a decline of dharma and a rise of adharma, I manifest myself… to protect the good, to destroy the wicked and to re-establish dharma, I appear age after age\" (Bhagavad Gita 4.7–8). The ten main avatars (Dashavatara) include Matsya (fish), Varaha (boar), Narasimha (man-lion), Rama, Krishna and Kalki, who is still to come.\n• Rama – prince of Ayodhya, hero of the Ramayana; the ideal of dharma as a son, husband and king; defeats the demon king Ravana to rescue Sita. Celebrated at Diwali and Rama Navami.\n• Krishna – teacher of the Bhagavad Gita, playful cowherd of Vrindavan, and object of loving devotion. Many Gaudiya Vaishnavas (including ISKCON) see Krishna not as one avatar but as the original Supreme Person, the source of all avatars (Bhagavata Purana 1.3.28: \"Krishna is the Lord himself\").\n\nWHY IT MATTERS TODAY\nDeities give Hindus a personal focus for puja, prayer and festivals. A British Hindu family may have murtis of Ganesha, Lakshmi and Krishna on its home shrine. Stories of the avatars teach moral values and courage, and many people choose an ishta-devata – a chosen, personal deity."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","title":"Matter (prakriti), the three qualities (tri-guna), illusion (maya) and cosmology","specFocus":"Matter (prakriti); the three qualities (tri-guna); illusion (maya); cosmology (Rig Veda 10.129.6-7); the cycle of four ages; many worlds and their diverse inhabitants.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"PRAKRITI (MATTER)\nPrakriti is the material nature or energy from which the physical universe, our bodies and even our minds are made. In Samkhya philosophy it is contrasted with purusha (pure consciousness, spirit). The Bhagavad Gita lists eight elements of Krishna's \"lower\" material energy: earth, water, fire, air, space (ether), mind, intellect and ego (7.4), and describes the souls as his \"higher\" energy (7.5). Hindus therefore see matter as real and as coming from God, but as different from the eternal self (atman), which is conscious.\n\nTHE TRI-GUNA (THREE QUALITIES)\nPrakriti works through three gunas (\"qualities\" or \"strands\"), which mix in different amounts in everything – food, actions, people and even worship (Bhagavad Gita chapters 14 and 17):\n• Sattva – goodness, purity, light, calm and wisdom (e.g. fresh vegetarian food, study, peaceful worship).\n• Rajas – passion, activity, desire and restlessness (e.g. ambition, spicy food, chasing wealth).\n• Tamas – darkness, ignorance, laziness and inertia (e.g. stale food, intoxication, oversleeping, cruelty).\n\"Sattva, rajas and tamas – these gunas, born of prakriti, bind the eternal embodied self to the body\" (Gita 14.5). Hindus try to increase sattva through diet, meditation and good company, and ultimately to go beyond all three gunas.\n\nMAYA (ILLUSION)\nMaya means \"illusion\" or \"the power that deceives\". Hindus differ on what it means:\n• Advaita Vedanta (Shankara): the world of separate things is an appearance superimposed on Brahman, like a rope mistaken for a snake in dim light. When ignorance is removed, only Brahman remains.\n• Vaishnava and other theistic traditions: the world is real, but maya is God's energy that makes souls forget their true identity and think \"I am this body\" and \"this is mine\". The world is temporary, not unreal. Krishna says, \"This divine maya of mine, made of the gunas, is hard to overcome; but those who surrender to me cross beyond it\" (Gita 7.14).\n\nCOSMOLOGY – RIG VEDA 10.129 (THE NASADIYA SUKTA)\nThe \"Hymn of Creation\" begins in a state when \"there was neither non-existence nor existence\". Verses 6–7 ask: \"Who really knows? Who here will proclaim it? From where was it born, from where this creation? The gods came later than the creation of this world… He who oversees it in the highest heaven, he surely knows – or perhaps he does not know.\" This shows humility and wonder about origins, and many Hindus see it as compatible with modern science such as the Big Bang. Other Hindu accounts describe Vishnu lying on the cosmic ocean, with Brahma emerging from a lotus from his navel to create.\n\nTHE CYCLE OF FOUR AGES (YUGAS)\nTime is cyclical. Each great cycle (maha-yuga, about 4.32 million years) has four ages of declining virtue, in the ratio 4:3:2:1:\n1. Satya (Krita) Yuga – the age of truth; dharma stands on four legs like a bull.\n2. Treta Yuga – virtue declines by a quarter (Rama's age).\n3. Dvapara Yuga – half virtue (Krishna's age).\n4. Kali Yuga – the present age of quarrel and decline; traditionally begun in 3102 BCE.\nA thousand maha-yugas form one \"day of Brahma\" (a kalpa), after which the universe is dissolved and recreated.\n\nMANY WORLDS AND THEIR INHABITANTS\nThe Puranas describe fourteen worlds (lokas): higher heavenly realms such as Svarga (Indra's heaven) and Brahmaloka, the earthly realm (Bhu-loka), and lower realms (patala). They are inhabited by diverse beings – devas (gods), asuras (demons), gandharvas (heavenly musicians), nagas (serpent beings), humans, animals and plants. All are souls within samsara, and the Gita teaches that even the highest material worlds are temporary (8.16). Many Hindus also believe in countless universes."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","title":"The atman; samsara, karma and moksha","specFocus":"The concept of atman, as individual, eternal inner self, distinct from material mind and body. Cycle of birth and death: samsara; moral action and reaction: the law of karma; types of liberation: moksha.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"ATMAN – THE ETERNAL SELF\nThe atman is the real, individual, eternal inner self (often translated \"soul\"). It is conscious, unchanging and indestructible, and it is distinct from the material body and also from the material mind, intellect and ego, which are made of prakriti. The Bhagavad Gita teaches: \"The self is never born, nor does it ever die… it is unborn, eternal, ever-existing and primeval; it is not killed when the body is killed\" (2.20). The Katha Upanishad compares the self to a passenger in a chariot: the body is the chariot, the intellect the driver, the mind the reins and the senses the horses (Katha Upanishad 1.3.3–4).\nTraditions differ: in Advaita Vedanta the atman is ultimately identical with Brahman (\"Tat tvam asi\", Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7); in Dvaita and Vishishtadvaita the atman is eternally distinct from God, a tiny spark or part of him (Gita 15.7), whose nature is to serve and love God.\n\nSAMSARA – THE CYCLE OF BIRTH AND DEATH\nSamsara is the continuous cycle of birth, death and rebirth (reincarnation) through which the atman passes. \"As a person puts on new garments, giving up old ones, the self accepts new bodies, giving up the old and useless ones\" (Gita 2.22). The atman can be reborn as a human, animal, plant or in other worlds, depending on karma. Samsara is seen as a state of bondage and suffering, driven by desire and ignorance.\n\nKARMA – MORAL ACTION AND REACTION\nKarma literally means \"action\". The law of karma teaches that every intentional action has consequences that return to the person who performed it, in this life or a future one. \"As one acts, so one becomes: by good action one becomes good, by bad action bad\" (Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 4.4.5). Karma is not fate or punishment by God: it is a moral law of cause and effect, and people can change their future by their present choices. Many Hindus distinguish:\n• sanchita karma – the store of accumulated past karma;\n• prarabdha karma – the portion now \"ripening\" in this life (e.g. circumstances of birth);\n• kriyamana karma – the new karma being made now.\nGood karma (punya) leads to happier births; bad karma (papa) to suffering. However, even good karma keeps the soul in samsara, so the ultimate goal is to go beyond karma.\n\nMOKSHA – LIBERATION\nMoksha is liberation (release) from samsara, the final aim of human life. Hindus have different understandings of it:\n• Advaita: the atman realises its identity with nirguna Brahman, like a drop of water merging with the ocean; individuality as a separate self ends.\n• Vaishnava/theistic: the soul is freed from material bodies and enters the spiritual world (e.g. Vaikuntha or Goloka) to live in eternal loving relationship with God, keeping its individual identity. Traditional texts list types of liberation, including living in God's abode (salokya), being near him (samipya), having a form like his (sarupya) and merging into him (sayujya).\n• Jivanmukti – some believe a person can be liberated while still living (a jivanmukta).\nMoksha is reached through the paths of yoga (knowledge, action, meditation, devotion), and many believe it depends on God's grace. Krishna says that those who come to him \"do not take birth again\" (Gita 8.15).\n\nTODAY\nBelief in karma and rebirth influences daily choices: many Hindus avoid harming animals, give to charity and accept hardships calmly. At death, family members chant God's names and, after cremation, many take the ashes to a sacred river such as the Ganges; some British Hindus scatter ashes at designated sites on UK rivers."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","title":"Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","specFocus":"Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"INDIVIDUAL FREE WILL\nHindus believe that each atman has free will – the ability to choose how to act. The law of karma only makes sense if people are responsible for their choices. Past karma shapes the situation a person is born into (prarabdha karma), but within that situation they are free to choose good or bad actions, which create new karma (kriyamana). A common image is a game of cards: the hand you are dealt is your past karma, but how you play it is your free will.\nThe Bhagavad Gita stresses personal responsibility: \"Let a person lift themselves up by the self; let them not degrade themselves. The self alone is the friend of the self, and the self alone is the enemy of the self\" (6.5). At the end of his teaching Krishna tells Arjuna, \"Reflect on this fully, then do as you wish\" (18.63) – God does not force anyone.\nThere are debates. Some Hindus stress that the gunas and the senses strongly push a person (Gita 3.27, 3.33), so freedom has to be developed through self-control. Some theistic Hindus believe God, within the heart, guides and ultimately controls all things, while still giving souls freedom to turn towards him or away.\n\nRESPONSES TO SUFFERING\nHindus offer several explanations of and responses to suffering (dukkha):\n• Karma – much suffering is the result of a person's own past actions, so it is not random or unjust.\n• Ignorance and attachment – suffering comes from identifying with the temporary body and desiring temporary things (Gita 2.62–63: from dwelling on sense objects comes attachment, then desire, anger, delusion and ruin).\n• Suffering as a teacher – hardship can produce humility, compassion and a desire for God. Kunti, in the Bhagavata Purana (1.8.25), prays for difficulties so that she will always remember Krishna.\n• Equanimity – the Gita teaches tolerating pleasure and pain like the passing seasons (2.14) and remaining the same \"in happiness and distress\".\n• Practical compassion – suffering in others calls for action: seva (selfless service), charity and ahimsa. Gandhi's work for the poor shows this.\nHindus therefore respond with acceptance, prayer and puja, fasting or pilgrimage, and with service to those who suffer.\n\nKNOWLEDGE AND IGNORANCE\nAvidya (ignorance) is seen as the root cause of samsara and suffering. It is not lack of information but misunderstanding who we are – thinking \"I am the body\" instead of recognising the eternal atman. Maya fuels this ignorance.\nVidya or jnana (knowledge) is spiritual wisdom that removes ignorance \"as the sun dispels darkness\" (Gita 5.16). \"There is no purifier in this world like knowledge\" (Gita 4.38). The famous prayer of the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad (1.3.28) asks: \"Lead me from the unreal to the real, from darkness to light, from death to immortality.\"\n• Advaitins see knowledge (realising atman = Brahman) as the direct means of moksha.\n• Vaishnavas see true knowledge as knowing oneself as a servant of God, which naturally leads to devotion.\nKnowledge is gained from scripture (shruti and smriti), a qualified guru and personal practice such as meditation.\n\nTODAY\nThese beliefs encourage British Hindus to take responsibility for their choices, to respond to suffering with resilience and service (e.g. Sewa Day volunteering), and to study scripture in temple classes or at home."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","title":"Personal virtues including ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love","specFocus":"Personal virtues including ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind/sense control, humility, love.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"WHY VIRTUES MATTER\nHindus believe that developing good qualities purifies the mind, increases sattva, produces good karma and prepares the atman for moksha. The Bhagavad Gita lists \"divine qualities\" such as fearlessness, purity, self-control, non-violence, truthfulness, freedom from anger, compassion, gentleness and humility (16.1–3), and describes the qualities of a true devotee who is \"free from hatred, friendly and compassionate to all\" (12.13). The specification names six virtues.\n\nAHIMSA (NON-VIOLENCE)\nAhimsa means not harming any living being in thought, word or action. It follows from the belief that the atman is present in all beings and that God dwells in every heart. The Gita teaches that the wise \"see with equal vision a learned brahmin, a cow, an elephant, a dog and an outcaste\" (5.18). Ahimsa leads many Hindus to vegetarianism and opposition to cruelty. Mahatma Gandhi made ahimsa the basis of non-violent resistance (satyagraha) against British rule. Hindus debate whether ahimsa allows self-defence or a just war; the Gita is set on a battlefield and Krishna tells Arjuna to fight as his kshatriya duty, which some interpret literally and others (including Gandhi) symbolically.\n\nRESPECT\nRespect for others, especially parents, elders, teachers and guests, is a key virtue. The Taittiriya Upanishad (1.11.2) teaches: \"Treat your mother as God, treat your father as God, treat your teacher as God, treat your guest as God.\" Hindus show respect by touching the feet of elders (pranam), greeting with \"namaste\" (recognising the divine in the other), and caring for elderly relatives at home. Respect extends to all religions; Hindus often stress that there are many paths to God.\n\nEMPATHY\nEmpathy means feeling and understanding the joys and sufferings of others as if they were one's own. The Gita says the highest yogi is one who \"sees the pleasure and pain of all beings as if they were their own\" (6.32). Empathy grows from seeing the same atman in everyone, and it motivates seva and charity.\n\nMIND AND SENSE CONTROL\nSelf-discipline over the mind and the senses (indriyas) is essential for spiritual life. \"When a person withdraws the senses from sense objects, as a tortoise draws in its limbs, their wisdom is steady\" (Gita 2.58). The Katha Upanishad compares the senses to horses that must be controlled by the reins of the mind and the driver (intellect). Practices such as fasting, meditation, japa, celibacy for monks and avoiding intoxicants train self-control. \"For one who has conquered the mind, the mind is the best friend\" (Gita 6.6).\n\nHUMILITY\nHumility means freedom from pride and ego (ahankara). The Gita lists \"humility, freedom from pride, non-violence, tolerance and simplicity\" as the first qualities of knowledge (13.8). Bowing before the murti, serving others, and honouring the guru express humility. The 16th-century saint Chaitanya taught that one should be \"more humble than a blade of grass and more tolerant than a tree\" when chanting God's names.\n\nLOVE\nLove (prema) for God and for all beings is the heart of bhakti. Krishna says, \"Whoever offers me with love a leaf, a flower, a fruit or water, I accept that offering of love\" (Gita 9.26). Love of God naturally becomes love for all creatures in whom God lives. Saints such as Mirabai and Tulsidas expressed this love in poetry and song.\n\nTODAY\nThese virtues shape Hindu life in Britain: vegetarian temple kitchens, multigenerational families, respectful greetings, volunteering, and interfaith work (e.g. Hindu temples taking part in Interfaith Week)."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper1-1-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","title":"The four aims of human life; the meaning of dharma, sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma","specFocus":"The four aims of human life: dharma, artha, kama, moksha. The meaning of dharma; sanatana dharma; varnashrama dharma.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"THE FOUR AIMS OF HUMAN LIFE (PURUSHARTHAS)\nHindu tradition teaches four legitimate goals for human life:\n1. Dharma – duty, righteousness and moral order; living rightly according to one's nature and stage of life.\n2. Artha – wealth, prosperity and success, earned honestly to support family and society.\n3. Kama – pleasure and enjoyment, including love, beauty, art and family life, enjoyed within the limits of dharma.\n4. Moksha – liberation from samsara, the ultimate and highest aim.\nDharma frames the other three: wealth and pleasure are good when pursued according to dharma. Krishna says, \"I am desire that is not contrary to dharma\" (Gita 7.11). Moksha is the final goal that gives meaning to the others. The aims are often linked to the four ashramas (stages of life): brahmacharya (student – learning dharma), grihastha (householder – artha and kama within dharma), vanaprastha (retirement – detachment) and sannyasa (renunciation – seeking moksha).\n\nTHE MEANING OF DHARMA\nDharma comes from the root \"dhri\", \"to uphold\" or \"to sustain\". It is the moral and cosmic order that holds everything together, and also each person's duty within that order. There is no single English equivalent: it can mean duty, law, religion, righteousness or the essential nature of something (it is the dharma of fire to burn). Sources of dharma include the Vedas, smriti texts such as the Manusmriti, the example of good people and one's conscience.\n\nSANATANA DHARMA\nSanatana dharma means \"eternal dharma\" or \"eternal way\" and is the name many Hindus prefer for their religion. It refers to universal, timeless principles that apply to everyone – truthfulness, non-violence, purity, self-control, compassion – and, in many traditions, to the soul's eternal nature as a part of, and servant of, God. These universal duties are sometimes called sadharana dharma.\n\nVARNASHRAMA DHARMA\nVarnashrama dharma is duty according to one's varna (social class) and ashrama (stage of life).\n• The four varnas, described in the Purusha Sukta (Rig Veda 10.90.12) as coming from the mouth, arms, thighs and feet of the cosmic Purusha, are brahmins (priests and teachers), kshatriyas (rulers and soldiers), vaishyas (merchants and farmers) and shudras (workers and servants).\n• The Gita teaches that the varnas were created \"according to the divisions of guna and karma\" (4.13) – qualities and work – which many Hindus interpret as meaning that varna should depend on character and ability, not birth.\n• \"It is better to perform one's own dharma imperfectly than another's well\" (Gita 3.35) – Arjuna, a kshatriya, should fight, not become a monk.\nVarna is not the same as jati (caste by birth into a hereditary group). Over time the system became rigid and hereditary, and groups outside it (today called Dalits) suffered \"untouchability\". Reformers such as Gandhi, Swami Vivekananda and the Arya Samaj opposed caste discrimination; untouchability was abolished in the Indian Constitution (1950). In Britain there has been debate about caste discrimination law, and many British Hindus see caste as having little importance today, though it may still influence some marriages.\n\nDIVERSITY AND TODAY\nSome Hindus see varnashrama as an ideal spiritual-social order if based on qualities; others reject it entirely. Most British Hindus follow the four aims by working hard and honestly, enjoying family life and festivals, worshipping and giving to charity, and keeping moksha as their ultimate goal."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","title":"Places of worship and their importance: home; temple; outdoors (such as shrines); the space of the heart","specFocus":"Places of worship and their importance: home; temple; outdoors (such as shrines); the space of the heart.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"OVERVIEW\nHindus believe God is everywhere, so worship can happen anywhere. The specification names four places: the home, the temple, outdoors (such as shrines) and the space of the heart.\n\nTHE HOME\nFor most Hindus the home is the main place of daily worship. Almost every Hindu home has a shrine (often called the mandir or puja room), which may be a whole room, a shelf or a small cabinet. It contains murtis or pictures of chosen deities (e.g. Ganesha, Lakshmi, Krishna, Shiva), a lamp (diva), incense, a bell, flowers and a tray for offerings. Worship is often led by the mother or grandmother, and families perform puja and arati in the morning and evening, offer food before eating, and pray before exams or journeys. Many key rites of passage (samskaras), such as the naming ceremony, take place at home. Home worship passes faith to children and makes the whole house sacred; God is treated as an honoured guest (\"atithi devo bhava\", Taittiriya Upanishad 1.11.2).\n\nTHE TEMPLE (MANDIR)\nA temple is believed to be the home of God on earth. In a traditional temple, murtis are installed by the prana pratishtha ceremony, after which the deity is believed to be present. The inner sanctum (garbhagriha, \"womb-house\") is under the tallest tower (shikhara in north India; gopuram gateways are prominent in south Indian temples). Worshippers remove shoes, ring a bell to announce their arrival, receive darshan, offer flowers or money, join arati and receive prasad (blessed food). Priests (pujaris) perform the main rituals, often several times daily. Temples are also community centres: they host festivals, weddings, language and dance classes, youth groups and free meals. In Britain there are well over 150 Hindu temples, such as BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir in Neasden (London, opened 1995, a traditional stone mandir), Bhaktivedanta Manor (ISKCON, Watford) and Shri Venkateswara (Balaji) Temple at Tividale near Birmingham. Many began in converted buildings such as churches or warehouses.\nVisiting a temple is not compulsory; some Hindus attend mainly at festivals.\n\nOUTDOORS (SUCH AS SHRINES)\nIn India there are countless outdoor shrines: at the foot of sacred trees (e.g. the peepal), by roadsides, on hills and beside rivers. The River Ganges, mountains such as Kailash and Govardhan Hill, and places linked to the deities are themselves treated as sacred. Many festivals happen outdoors – Holi bonfires, immersing Ganesha murtis in water, or bathing at the Kumbh Mela. In Britain some Hindus worship at rivers or the sea, for example during Ganesh Chaturthi immersions or when scattering ashes.\n\nTHE SPACE OF THE HEART\nHindus believe God lives in the heart of every being as Paramatma: \"The Lord dwells in the hearts of all beings\" (Gita 18.61). The Chandogya Upanishad (8.1.1–3) describes a small lotus-like \"space within the heart\" inside the \"city of Brahman\" (the body), as vast as the universe. So the most important place of worship is internal: through meditation, japa and remembering God, a person can worship anywhere, at any time. Yogis and many Advaitins emphasise this inner worship.\n\nIMPORTANCE AND DIVERSITY\nPlaces of worship help Hindus focus the mind, receive blessings, build community and pass on traditions. Some, such as the Arya Samaj, reject murti worship and worship around the sacred fire (havan) instead. Many Hindus see outer worship as preparation for the inner worship of the heart."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","title":"Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance; Shaivism and Vaishnavism","specFocus":"Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance: havan, puja, arati, darshan, bhajan/kirtan, japa/mantra; key differences in worship in Shaivism and Vaishnavism and different Hindu views about the importance of worship.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"FORMS OF WORSHIP AND MEDITATION\n• Havan (homa/yajna) – the ancient Vedic fire ceremony. A fire is lit in a small fire-pit (havan kund) and offerings of ghee, grains, herbs and wood are made into it while Vedic mantras are chanted. Agni, the fire, is seen as the messenger who carries offerings to God. Havan is performed at weddings (the couple walk around the sacred fire), house-warmings and festivals, and it is central for the Arya Samaj. Significance: purification, connection with the Vedic tradition, God as witness.\n• Puja – worship with offerings, at home or in the temple. The worshipper honours the deity like a guest, offering items such as water, flowers, incense, light, food and sometimes bathing and dressing the murti (traditionally sixteen steps, the shodashopachara). Significance: expressing love and gratitude and receiving blessings. \"Whoever offers me with love a leaf, a flower, a fruit or water, I accept\" (Gita 9.26).\n• Arati – a lamp ceremony. A tray holding lamps with ghee-soaked wicks is waved in circles before the murti while a bell rings and a song (e.g. \"Om Jai Jagdish Hare\") is sung. Afterwards worshippers pass their hands over the flame and then over their eyes and head, receiving the deity's blessing and light. Significance: God as light that removes darkness and ignorance.\n• Darshan – \"seeing\". Worshippers stand before the murti to see the deity and, crucially, to be seen by God. It is a two-way encounter. People also seek darshan of holy people and sacred places.\n• Bhajan and kirtan – devotional singing. Bhajans are hymns, often sung in groups; kirtan is usually call-and-response chanting of God's names with drums (mridanga) and cymbals (kartals). Significance: expressing and increasing love of God (bhakti); the Bhagavata Purana (12.3.51) teaches that chanting God's names brings liberation in Kali Yuga.\n• Japa / mantra – repeating a mantra (sacred sound or prayer), often silently or softly on a mala of 108 beads. Examples: \"Om\"; the Gayatri Mantra (Rig Veda 3.62.10); \"Om Namah Shivaya\"; the Hare Krishna maha-mantra. \"Among sacrifices I am japa\" (Gita 10.25). Significance: focusing and purifying the mind, meditation on God.\n\nSHAIVISM AND VAISHNAVISM – KEY DIFFERENCES IN WORSHIP\n• Focus: Shaivas worship Shiva (often with Parvati, Ganesha and Murugan); Vaishnavas worship Vishnu and his avatars, especially Krishna and Rama, with Lakshmi or Radha.\n• Representation: Shiva is mainly worshipped through the lingam, a smooth rounded stone representing his formless, infinite nature; Vishnu/Krishna is usually worshipped in a fully human-like murti, dressed and decorated.\n• Offerings and rituals: Shaivas pour water, milk or yoghurt over the lingam (abhisheka) and offer bilva (bael) leaves; Vaishnavas offer tulsi leaves and vegetarian food to the deity, which becomes prasad.\n• Marks and beads: Shaivas wear three horizontal lines of ash (vibhuti/tripundra) and rudraksha beads; Vaishnavas wear a vertical \"U\"-shaped tilak and tulsi beads.\n• Mantras and festivals: \"Om Namah Shivaya\", Maha Shivaratri (night-long vigil, fasting); the Hare Krishna maha-mantra, Janmashtami and Rama Navami.\n• Emphasis: Shaivism has a strong tradition of meditation, yoga and asceticism; Vaishnavism stresses bhakti, temple service, kirtan and surrender to God's grace.\n\nDIFFERENT VIEWS ABOUT THE IMPORTANCE OF WORSHIP\n• For bhakti traditions, worship is the heart of religion and itself the goal: loving service of God continues even in liberation.\n• For many Advaitins, ritual worship is useful for purifying the mind but is a lower stage; knowledge (jnana) is the direct path to moksha.\n• Reform movements such as the Arya Samaj reject murti worship and focus on havan and the Vedas.\n• Some Hindus see ethical living, seva or meditation as more important than ritual, and many British Hindus worship mainly at festivals."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","title":"Focuses of worship and representations of the divine and their importance","specFocus":"Focuses of worship and representations of the divine and their importance: one God (personal or non-personal); the many deities, guru and other elders; holy land, hills and rivers; sacred plants and animals; the murti as a representation of God.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"FOCUSES OF WORSHIP\nHindus direct their worship towards many focuses, all of which can be seen as ways of approaching the one divine reality.\n\nONE GOD (PERSONAL OR NON-PERSONAL)\nMany Hindus focus worship on one Supreme God. For a Vaishnava this may be Krishna or Vishnu; for a Shaiva, Shiva; for a Shakta, the Goddess. Others worship God as non-personal Brahman, often through the sacred syllable Om (Aum), which the Mandukya Upanishad describes as representing all of reality, past, present and future. Meditation on Om is a way of worshipping the formless divine.\n\nTHE MANY DEITIES, GURU AND OTHER ELDERS\n• Deities – Hindus worship deities such as Ganesha, Lakshmi, Hanuman, Saraswati and Durga for their particular qualities and blessings. Many families have a chosen deity (ishta-devata) and a family deity (kula-devata). Many see the deities as aspects or servants of the one God (Rig Veda 1.164.46).\n• Guru – a spiritual teacher who removes the darkness of ignorance (\"gu\" = darkness, \"ru\" = remover). Disciples honour the guru with deep respect, touching their feet and celebrating Guru Purnima. \"Approach a spiritual master, enquire submissively and render service; the wise who have seen the truth will teach you\" (Gita 4.34). Some gurus, such as the leaders of the Swaminarayan movement, are honoured as especially close to God.\n• Elders – parents, grandparents and teachers are honoured as God: \"Treat your mother as God, your father as God, your teacher as God\" (Taittiriya Upanishad 1.11.2). Hindus touch elders' feet to receive blessings.\n\nHOLY LAND, HILLS AND RIVERS\nIndia (Bharat) is regarded by many Hindus as a sacred land, filled with places linked to the deities and avatars. Mountains such as Kailash (the abode of Shiva) and Govardhan Hill (which Krishna lifted to protect the villagers of Vraj) are worshipped; pilgrims walk around Govardhan (parikrama). Rivers are worshipped as goddesses, especially the Ganga (Ganges), believed to flow from Vishnu's feet through Shiva's hair and to purify sins; the Yamuna and Saraswati are also sacred. The Ganga arati at Varanasi and Haridwar is performed every evening.\n\nSACRED PLANTS AND ANIMALS\n• Plants – Tulsi (holy basil) is worshipped as a goddess dear to Vishnu and Krishna and grown in many Hindu homes; bilva leaves are offered to Shiva; the peepal/ashvattha tree is sacred (\"Among trees I am the ashvattha\", Gita 10.26); the lotus symbolises purity.\n• Animals – the cow is honoured as a mother (gau mata) because she gives milk and represents non-violent generosity; Krishna is a cowherd (Govinda). Animals are linked with deities as their vehicles (vahanas): Nandi the bull (Shiva), Garuda the eagle (Vishnu), the mouse (Ganesha), the swan (Saraswati), the lion or tiger (Durga). Hanuman is honoured as a monkey devotee, and the snake (naga) is honoured at Naga Panchami.\n\nTHE MURTI AS A REPRESENTATION OF GOD\nA murti is a sacred image or form of a deity, made of stone, metal, wood or other material, often according to traditional rules. In a temple, the prana pratishtha ceremony invites the deity's presence into it. Views differ:\n• Many Vaishnavas believe God is truly present in the murti (arca-avatara), so it is served like a living person: woken, bathed, dressed, fed and put to rest.\n• Others see the murti as a symbol and aid to concentration, pointing to a God beyond form.\n• The Arya Samaj rejects murti worship as not Vedic.\nMurti features carry meaning: Ganesha's large ears (listening), Shiva's third eye (wisdom), Lakshmi's lotus (purity), Vishnu's conch and discus (protection)."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-hinduism-paper2-2-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.4","title":"Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today: Diwali and Holi","specFocus":"Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today, including the origins and meaning of: Diwali; Holi.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"WHY FESTIVALS MATTER\nFestivals (utsavas) mark sacred events, honour the deities, retell scripture stories and bring families and communities together. For Hindus in Great Britain – a minority community – they are especially important for passing on faith and culture to young people and for sharing Hindu traditions with the wider public. The specification requires the origins and meaning of Diwali and Holi.\n\nDIWALI (DEEPAVALI) – \"ROW OF LIGHTS\"\nWhen: the new-moon night of the month of Kartik (October/November), usually as part of a five-day festival.\nOrigins (different regions emphasise different stories):\n• The return of Rama and Sita to Ayodhya after fourteen years of exile and the defeat of the demon king Ravana, told in the Ramayana. The people lit rows of lamps to welcome them home.\n• The worship of Lakshmi, goddess of wealth and good fortune, who is believed to visit homes that are clean and lit.\n• Krishna's defeat of the demon Narakasura (celebrated as Naraka Chaturdashi, especially in South India).\n• For many Gujarati Hindus, the day after Diwali is the Hindu New Year (Annakut/Govardhan Puja), when temples offer a \"mountain\" of food.\nMeaning: the victory of light over darkness, good over evil and knowledge over ignorance (\"Lead me from darkness to light\", Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.3.28); a fresh start; prosperity.\nPractices: cleaning and decorating homes; lighting divas (clay oil lamps) and fairy lights; drawing rangoli patterns at doorways; Lakshmi puja; new clothes; giving sweets (mithai) and gifts; fireworks; businesses opening new account books; visiting the temple and family.\nIn Great Britain: Leicester's Diwali celebrations on the Golden Mile (Belgrave Road) are among the largest outside India, with a lights switch-on attracting tens of thousands; Trafalgar Square in London hosts \"Diwali on the Square\"; Neasden Mandir's Annakut displays hundreds of vegetarian dishes. Schools and workplaces increasingly mark Diwali, and the Prime Minister has hosted Diwali receptions.\n\nHOLI – THE FESTIVAL OF COLOURS\nWhen: the full moon of the month of Phalgun (February/March), marking the arrival of spring.\nOrigins:\n• Prahlad and Holika: the demon king Hiranyakashipu demanded to be worshipped, but his son Prahlad remained devoted to Vishnu. His aunt Holika, believed to be immune to fire, sat with Prahlad in a bonfire, but she was burned while Prahlad was protected. Vishnu then appeared as Narasimha (man-lion) to destroy Hiranyakashipu. (The story of Prahlad is told in the Bhagavata Purana, book 7.) The night before Holi, bonfires (Holika Dahan) are lit to celebrate the victory of devotion over evil.\n• Krishna and Radha: the young Krishna playfully threw coloured water and powder over Radha and the gopis (cowherd girls) of Vrindavan; the playful spraying of colour recalls this divine love.\nMeaning: triumph of good over evil and of faith over pride; God's protection of devotees; the joy of spring and new life; equality – barriers of age, caste, gender and wealth are forgotten as everyone is covered in colour; forgiveness and renewing friendships.\nPractices: Holika bonfires; throwing coloured powder (gulal) and water; singing and dancing; sharing sweets and drinks (e.g. gujiya, thandai); visiting friends and family.\nIn Great Britain: temples and community groups hold Holi events, and Bhaktivedanta Manor and other temples host festivals of colours; local councils and universities hold public Holi celebrations open to all.\n\nDIVERSITY\nFestivals are celebrated differently by region and tradition: Bengali Hindus worship Kali at Diwali; Vaishnavas emphasise Krishna at Holi; some Hindus celebrate quietly at home, others at large public events."}],"rubrics":{"Beliefs":{"1":"1-mark questions (AO1). Either a multiple-choice question (choose the correct letter from four options) or a 'Give one…' question. One accurate answer gains the mark; no development is needed. Spell key terms correctly.","12":"12-mark 'Evaluate this statement' questions (AO2) – refer to Hindu teaching, give reasoned arguments for the statement, give reasoned arguments for a different view, reach a justified conclusion. Level 4 (10–12): well-argued, reasoned consideration of different points of view; logical chains of reasoning leading to judgements supported by knowledge of relevant evidence; references to religion applied to the issue. Level 3 (7–9): reasoned consideration of different views; logical chains of reasoning; clear reference to religion. Level 2 (4–6): reasoned consideration of one view, or recognition of different views each supported by reasons – maximum Level 2 if there is no reference to religion. Level 1 (1–3): a point of view with reason(s). Up to 3 extra SPaG marks are awarded on the 12-mark questions in the Beliefs section.","4":"4-mark 'Explain two ways…' / 'Explain two contrasting…' questions (AO1). For each of two ways: simple explanation of a relevant and accurate point = 1 mark; detailed explanation (developed with an example, consequence or further information) = 2 marks. Two separate points, each developed, for full marks. For 'contrasting' questions the two points must differ (e.g. Advaita and Vaishnava views).","6":"6-mark 'Explain two… Refer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer' questions (AO1). Each of two teachings: simple explanation = 1 mark, detailed explanation = 2 marks. Source of authority: 2 marks for naming AND applying a relevant source (e.g. Bhagavad Gita 2.22, Rig Veda 10.129, an Upanishad, a guru or acharya), or 1 mark for naming it without applying it, or applying it without naming it."},"Practices":{"1":"1-mark questions (AO1). Either a multiple-choice question (choose the correct letter from four options) or a 'Give one…' question. One accurate answer gains the mark; no development is needed.","12":"12-mark 'Evaluate this statement' questions (AO2). Level 4 (10–12): well-argued, reasoned consideration of different points of view with logical chains of reasoning leading to a justified judgement and religion applied to the issue. Level 3 (7–9): reasoned consideration of different views, clear reference to religion. Level 2 (4–6): one view reasoned, or different views simply supported; maximum Level 2 without reference to religion. Level 1 (1–3): a view with reasons. (In the exam, SPaG marks for Hinduism are awarded on the Beliefs section 12-mark questions.)","4":"4-mark 'Explain two ways…' / 'Explain two contrasting…' questions (AO1). For each of two ways: simple explanation of a relevant and accurate point = 1 mark; detailed explanation = 2 marks. Use examples from Hindu practice in Britain or India today to develop points.","6":"6-mark 'Explain two… Refer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer' questions (AO1). Each of two points: simple = 1 mark, detailed = 2 marks. Source of authority: 2 marks for naming and applying a relevant source, 1 mark for naming only or applying only. Sources can include the Bhagavad Gita, Vedas, Upanishads, Ramayana, Puranas, the Yoga Sutras or a respected guru."}},"markingSchema":{"dsl":"SCHEME AQA Religious Studies adaptable guidance\nCOMMAND ALL\nMARKS AUTO\nREQUIRE use accurate subject knowledge and answer the command word\nREQUIRE support religious or non-religious views where the question asks for them\nNEVER invent quotations, sources or official level descriptors\nFEEDBACK be specific, supportive and honest about missing content\n\nCOMMAND Explain\nMARKS AUTO\nAWARD 1 one accurate relevant point\nAWARD 1 a clear linked explanation\n\nCOMMAND Evaluate\nMARKS AUTO\nBAND 9-12 well-argued evaluation with a justified conclusion\nBAND 5-8 relevant discussion with some evaluation\nBAND 1-4 limited points with little evaluation","id":"aqa-rs-generic","references":[{"title":"AQA Religious Studies 8062 specification","url":"https://www.aqa.org.uk/subjects/religious-studies/gcse/religious-studies-8062/specification"},{"title":"AQA scheme of assessment","url":"https://www.aqa.org.uk/subjects/religious-studies/gcse/religious-studies-8062/specification/scheme-of-assessment"}],"sourceNotes":"AQA GCSE RS A uses 1/1/4/6/12 question structures and a separate SPaG overlay. Check the current official specification and mark scheme before relying on a mark estimate.","status":"adaptable","title":"AQA Religious Studies · check and adapt","version":1},"moduleCounts":{"paper1":7,"paper2":4},"meta":{"modulesTotal":11,"paper1QuestionCount":70,"paper2QuestionCount":40,"provenance":"Original practice questions, model answers and revision notes written fresh to the published AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062) specification (version 1.3, September 2025, section 3.1.4 Hinduism) and its assessment structure. Not past-paper material and not taken from mark schemes, textbooks or revision guides. Model answers are indicative exemplars, not official AQA mark schemes. Short quotations from Hindu scriptures are paraphrased translations for study purposes.","qualification":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062), Component 1: Hinduism (8062/14)","questionTotal":110,"title":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A – Hinduism"}});
